{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546893212,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3547e33002pAIJoZPZMZd5Mg","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d3546247001xncrW6xtXj21P3","type":"tool","id":"call_5660dce997a540a39c58f5cb","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs, lines 1-1148\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: One-Turn Command Behaviour Runner\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Command surfacing is only proven by a turn that starts the way an operator\n6: // starts it: one prompt, the real CLI, and a session id the transcript reader\n7: // can open afterwards. Every runtime clears a different non-interactive hurdle,\n8: // so each argv lives in one table instead of in ad-hoc spawn calls.\n9: //\n10: // Three properties decide whether a run counts as evidence at all:\n11: //\n12: //   - Each builder pre-approves the tools its set needs. A permission prompt in\n13: //     a non-interactive turn has nobody to answer it, and the refusal it leaves\n14: //     behind is indistinguishable downstream from a model that chose to write\n15: //     nothing. Without this, every must-not-start row passes for the wrong\n16: //     reason.\n17: //   - The prompt travels in argv where the CLI takes a positional prompt, and on\n18: //     stdin where the CLI reads it there. Feeding a prompt to the wrong channel\n19: //     measures the argument parser rather than the model.\n20: //   - A turn is capped by the prompt it runs. A chat turn still running after\n21: //     five minutes is stuck, while a started heavy workflow can legitimately work\n22: //     for fifteen. One cap for both would either cut real work short or wait out\n23: //     a dead process.\n24: //\n25: // A live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner also owns its clean-up. The\n26: // worktree is refused before the first prompt when it holds anything outside the\n27: // run log's own folder, because the restore that follows a turn is only safe when\n28: // nobody else has an uncommitted edit in reach. Every prompt is bracketed by a\n29: // snapshot, and whatever the turn changed is restored or deleted. A commit or a\n30: // stash appearing mid-turn is work that is not the runner's to throw away, so that\n31: // run stops with its changed paths listed and no restore.\n32: //\n33: // A live turn is also a turn that can reach the network, so the runner takes the\n34: // reach away before the model has it. The child gets none of the GitHub tokens, an\n35: // empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on.\n36: // A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout that has any git remote,\n37: // because a command wrongly started there could push, tag or publish something the\n38: // arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it keeps running in this\n39: // checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n40: //\n41: // Usage:\n42: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv <runtime>\n43: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --list <set>\n44: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)\n45: //     --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]\n46: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>\n47: \n48: import { appendFileSync, mkdirSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, realpathSync } from 'node:fs';\n49: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n50: import { homedir } from 'node:os';\n51: import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';\n52: import { spawn, spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n53: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n54: import { dshProjectKey, dshStoreRoot } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/dsh.mjs';\n55: import { backfillRunLog, briefDelivered, replayOffer } from './command-offer.mjs';\n56: import { restoreTurn, statusSnapshot } from './worktree-restore.mjs';\n57: \n58: const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n59: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(HERE, '../../../../../..');\n60: const DSH_PATCH_DIR = resolve(REPO_ROOT, '.dsh/patches');\n61: // A stored session is found by its id plus the directory the turn ran in, and the\n62: // scorer reads it back from another checkout, so the locator records the resolved\n63: // directory instead of letting each reader compare the id against its own root.\n64: const PROJECT_DIR = realpathSync(REPO_ROOT);\n65: const PROMPTS_PATH = resolve(HERE, 'command-prompts.jsonl');\n66: \n67: const EXIT_REFUSED = 2;\n68: const EXIT_HEAD_MOVED = 3;\n69: \n70: // Keyed by prompt kind rather than by set name, because one arm can hold rows\n71: // from either set and the cap has to follow the row, not the invocation.\n72: const CAP_MS = Object.freeze({\n73:   chat: 300_000,\n74:   started: 900_000,\n75: });\n76: \n77: const CLAUDE_MODEL = 'claude-sonnet-5-5';\n78: const CODEX_MODEL = 'gpt-6-luna';\n79: // The variant rides the model string, because `opencode run` has no flag for it.\n80: const OPENCODE_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017#max';\n81: const PI_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n82: const DEVIN_MODEL = 'swe-2-max';\n83: const DSH_MODEL = 'deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n84: \n85: // Runtimes the phase reports as not measured, with the reason a run would be\n86: // wasted work rather than evidence. Cursor's reader never leaves gated, so its\n87: // transcript could not be scored even if the turn ran.\n88: const NOT_MEASURED = Object.freeze({\n89:   cursor: 'its session reader is gated, so no produced transcript can be scored',\n90: });\n91: \n92: function has(object, key) {\n93:   return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(object, key);\n94: }\n95: \n96: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n97: // Runtime argv table\n98: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n99: \n100: const RUNTIMES = {\n101:   claude: {\n102:     command: 'claude',\n103:     model: CLAUDE_MODEL,\n104:     promptOnStdin: false,\n105:     // stream-json writes one JSON object per line and every one of them carries\n106:     // the session id the transcript is stored under.\n107:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'session_id' },\n108:     // The reader opens a transcript by id plus its own path, and Claude Code names\n109:     // the file after the session inside a folder per working directory.\n110:     findTranscript: claudeTranscriptPath,\n111:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n112:       '-p',\n113:       prompt,\n114:       '--model',\n115:       CLAUDE_MODEL,\n116:       // stream-json is the only output format that carries the session id the\n117:       // reader needs after the turn ends.\n118:       '--output-format',\n119:       'stream-json',\n120:       '--verbose',\n121:       // Print mode has nobody to answer an approval prompt, and the default mode\n122:       // both denies writes and refuses shell commands, which is exactly the\n123:       // behaviour an ask-row test must be free to observe.\n124:       '--permission-mode',\n125:       'bypassPermissions',\n126:     ],\n127:   },\n128:   codex: {\n129:     command: 'codex',\n130:     model: CODEX_MODEL,\n131:     promptOnStdin: true,\n132:     // The run opens with an event naming its thread, and the rollout file is\n133:     // named after that thread id.\n134:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'thread_id' },\n135:     // The rollout file is named after the thread id the stream reported, so the id\n136:     // alone leads back to it once the store has been searched.\n137:     findTranscript: codexTranscriptPath,\n138:     buildArgv: () => [\n139:       'exec',\n140:       // The JSON event stream is how a turn reports its thread id.\n141:       '--json',\n142:       '--model',\n143:       CODEX_MODEL,\n144:       '-c',\n145:       'model_reasoning_effort=medium',\n146:       '-c',\n147:       'service_tier=fast',\n148:       // A headless run cannot answer an approval prompt, and `exec` defaults to a\n149:       // read-only sandbox that would silently swallow every write.\n150:       '-c',\n151:       'approval_policy=never',\n152:       '--sandbox',\n153:       'workspace-write',\n154:       '-',\n155:     ],\n156:   },\n157:   opencode: {\n158:     command: 'opencode',\n159:     model: OPENCODE_MODEL,\n160:     promptOnStdin: false,\n161:     // The turn's own events carry the id of the session it created.\n162:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'sessionID' },\n163:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n164:       // `opencode run` takes no --dir, and the child already starts in the\n165:       // checkout, so passing the directory a second time would only fail.\n166:       'run',\n167:       // Without this the turn is handed to the operator's long-running background\n168:       // service, whose tools run with that service's environment. The hardened\n169:       // environment this runner builds would then never reach a tool call.\n170:       '--standalone',\n171:       '--model',\n172:       OPENCODE_MODEL,\n173:       '--format',\n174:       'json',\n175:       // Auto-approve everything not explicitly denied. A run that stalls on a\n176:       // permission prompt reads as a model that declined to act.\n177:       '--auto',\n178:       prompt,\n179:     ],\n180:   },\n181:   pi: {\n182:     command: 'pi',\n183:     model: PI_MODEL,\n184:     promptOnStdin: false,\n185:     // Print mode never reports the session it wrote, so the runner mints the id\n186:     // and passes it on the command line instead of recovering it from the stream.\n187:     locator: { kind: 'runner' },\n188:     buildArgv: ({ prompt, sessionId }) => [\n189:       '-p',\n190:       // Without it a non-interactive Pi probes the network on startup and can\n191:       // hang before it ever reads the prompt.\n192:       '--offline',\n193:       '--model',\n194:       PI_MODEL,\n195:       // Without a minted id the log would have to be guessed from whatever\n196:       // file is newest, which can open a neighbouring turn's transcript.\n197:       '--session-id',\n198:       sessionId,\n199:       prompt,\n200:     ],\n201:   },\n202:   devin: {\n203:     command: 'devin',\n204:     model: DEVIN_MODEL,\n205:     promptOnStdin: false,\n206:     // A Devin turn keeps its session in a store its own output does not name, so\n207:     // the id comes from the listing of the directory the turn ran in.\n208:     locator: { kind: 'listing', args: ['list', '--format', 'json'] },\n209:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n210:       '-p',\n211:       '--model',\n212:       DEVIN_MODEL,\n213:       // Under `accept-edits` every tool call that wants confirmation is refused,\n214:       // so a headless turn explores its whole budget and writes nothing.\n215:       '--permission-mode',\n216:       'dangerous',\n217:       // A fresh checkout has never been trusted interactively, and print mode has\n218:       // nobody to answer the trust prompt, so the turn would refuse to start.\n219:       '--respect-workspace-trust',\n220:       'false',\n221:       // No `--sandbox` here. That flag forces a permission mode that ignores\n222:       // `dangerous` and rejects every write in print mode, so a turn would write\n223:       // nothing and read as a model that declined. Containment comes from the\n224:       // arena instead.\n225:       // The separator keeps a prompt that opens with a dash from parsing as a flag.\n226:       '--',\n227:       prompt,\n228:     ],\n229:   },\n230:   dsh: {\n231:     command: 'dsh',\n232:     // dsh takes no model flag at all. The roster route patch decides the model,\n233:     // and this table pins the verified LLM Gateway route.\n234:     model: DSH_MODEL,\n235:     promptOnStdin: true,\n236:     // The harness names the session itself and refuses an id it never created,\n237:     // so the turn's id is recovered from the store folder its log lands in.\n238:     locator: { kind: 'store', list: dshSessionIds },\n239:     buildArgv: () => [\n240:       '--profile',\n241:       'headless',\n242:       '--patch',\n243:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'route-llmgateway-deepseek.patch.yml'),\n244:       // No lineage patch: it marks the run as a fan-out leaf, and the repo\n245:       // guards keep the prompt advisor off for a leaf. A chat turn has to\n246:       // receive the brief like a person's session would.\n247:       '--patch',\n248:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'guards.patch.yml'),\n249:       '--json',\n250:       '--permission-mode',\n251:       'workspace-write',\n252:       // Approvals default to ask, and a headless turn has nobody to answer.\n253:       '--approval-policy',\n254:       'never',\n255:       // The dash takes the prompt from stdin, which is the channel a headless\n256:       // run reads, and keeps a prompt that opens with one from parsing as a\n257:       // flag.\n258:       '-',\n259:     ],\n260:   },\n261: };\n262: \n263: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n264: // Session locator\n265: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n266: //\n267: // A run log is only evidence if it leads back to the exact transcript the turn\n268: // wrote, and each runtime exposes that handle in its own way. Three sources\n269: // exist: an id the CLI reports after the turn, either in its output or in the\n270: // session listing it keeps, an id the runner hands the CLI before it starts, or\n271: // no recorded source at all, which the log states rather than filling with the\n272: // newest file in the store.\n273: \n274: // Output arrives in chunks that cut a line in half, so a line is parsed only\n275: // once its newline has come through. Parsing stops at the first match, because\n276: // the id never changes within one turn.\n277: function streamLocator(field) {\n278:   let pending = '';\n279:   let captured = null;\n280:   return {\n281:     push(chunk) {\n282:       if (field === null || captured !== null) return;\n283:       pending += chunk;\n284:       const lines = pending.split('\\n');\n285:       pending = lines.pop() ?? '';\n286:       for (const line of lines) {\n287:         captured = readId(line, field);\n288:         if (captured !== null) return;\n289:       }\n290:     },\n291:     value() {\n292:       if (captured === null && pending !== '') captured = readId(pending, field);\n293:       return captured;\n294:     },\n295:   };\n296: }\n297: \n298: // A line that is not a JSON object is ordinary chatter, so a parse failure says\n299: // this line holds no locator rather than that the run failed.\n300: function readId(line, field) {\n301:   let event;\n302:   try {\n303:     event = JSON.parse(line);\n304:   } catch {\n305:     return null;\n306:   }\n307:   const id = event?.[field];\n308:   return typeof id === 'string' && id !== '' ? id : null;\n309: }\n310: \n311: // A runtime that keeps its sessions in a store its output never names still lists\n312: // them for the directory the turn ran in, so two listings taken around a turn\n313: // reduce the store to a set difference. The listing runs in the child's\n314: // environment, because that is the store the turn itself wrote to.\n315: const LISTING_CAP_MS = 60_000;\n316: \n317: function listingIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n318:   const source = runtime.locator;\n319:   if (source.kind !== 'listing') return null;\n320:   const result = spawnSync(runtime.command, source.args, {\n321:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n322:     env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n323:     encoding: 'utf8',\n324:     timeout: LISTING_CAP_MS,\n325:   });\n326:   if (result.error) return null;\n327:   return result.status === 0 ? listedSessionIds(result.stdout) : null;\n328: }\n329: \n330: // A runtime that names its session only in a store it owns is bracketed by two\n331: // readings of that store, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote.\n332: function listsStore(runtime) {\n333:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'listing' || runtime.locator.kind === 'store';\n334: }\n335: \n336: // Only the reading differs between the store-backed forms: one CLI answers with\n337: // its own index and the other keeps one folder per session. Any other kind\n338: // names its session in the turn's output instead.\n339: function storeIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n340:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'store'\n341:     ? runtime.locator.list()\n342:     : listingIds(runtime, ghConfig);\n343: }\n344: \n345: // The listing is a JSON array of session objects whose `id` field is the value\n346: // the CLI's own resume takes, so that field is the one that leads back to a\n347: // transcript. Any other shape names no session, which the caller reads as no id\n348: // rather than as a failed run.\n349: function listedSessionIds(text) {\n350:   let entries;\n351:   try {\n352:     entries = JSON.parse(text);\n353:   } catch {\n354:     return null;\n355:   }\n356:   if (!Array.isArray(entries)) return null;\n357:   return entries\n358:     .map((entry) => (entry !== null && typeof entry === 'object' ? entry.id : null))\n359:     .filter((id) => typeof id === 'string' && id !== '');\n360: }\n361: \n362: // A session another operator opened while the turn ran is not this turn's, and a\n363: // listing that reports none or several added entries has no id it can name with\n364: // confidence, so only a single added entry becomes the locator.\n365: function addedSessionId(before, after) {\n366:   if (before === null || after === null) return null;\n367:   const known = new Set(before);\n368:   const added = [...new Set(after.filter((id) => !known.has(id)))];\n369:   return added.length === 1 ? added[0] : null;\n370: }\n371: \n372: // DSH keeps its session logs under DSH_HOME, which an operator can move, so the\n373: // locator records the home the run used and the transcript stays findable from\n374: // the log alone.\n375: function dshHome() {\n376:   return process.env.DSH_HOME || join(homedir(), '.dsh');\n377: }\n378: \n379: // A DSH session is a folder named after its id under one folder per project the\n380: // harness keyed from the directory the turn ran in, and the turn's own output\n381: // never names that id. The store root comes from the reader so the folder listed\n382: // here is the folder the reader will search. A store the harness has not created\n383: // yet lists nothing, which is the same answer as a turn that wrote no session.\n384: function dshSessionIds() {\n385:   const store = join(dshStoreRoot(), dshProjectKey(PROJECT_DIR));\n386:   try {\n387:     return readdirSync(store, { withFileTypes: true })\n388:       .filter((entry) => entry.isDirectory())\n389:       .map((entry) => entry.name);\n390:   } catch {\n391:     return [];\n392:   }\n393: }\n394: \n395: // A store folder is the CLI's to lay out, so the walk follows the folders it\n396: // creates and matches on the file name instead of predicting a path. A store that\n397: // is missing or unreadable names no transcript, which is the same answer as a turn\n398: // that wrote none rather than a reason to fail the run.\n399: function findTranscriptFiles(dir, matches, depth) {\n400:   let entries;\n401:   try {\n402:     entries = readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true });\n403:   } catch {\n404:     return [];\n405:   }\n406:   const found = [];\n407:   for (const entry of entries) {\n408:     if (entry.isFile() && matches(entry.name)) {\n409:       found.push(join(dir, entry.name));\n410:     } else if (entry.isDirectory() && depth > 0) {\n411:       found.push(...findTranscriptFiles(join(dir, entry.name), matches, depth - 1));\n412:     }\n413:   }\n414:   return found;\n415: }\n416: \n417: // One match is the turn's own transcript, while two leave the locator without a\n418: // path because choosing between them could score a neighbouring turn under this\n419: // row. The path is resolved so a log written from another checkout still opens.\n420: function storedTranscriptPath(root, matches, depth = Infinity) {\n421:   const found = findTranscriptFiles(root, matches, depth);\n422:   return found.length === 1 ? resolve(found[0]) : null;\n423: }\n424: \n425: // Claude Code keeps one folder per working directory and writes each transcript\n426: // directly inside it, so one level below the store is the whole of the search. A\n427: // helper transcript sits deeper inside `subagents/`, which belongs to another turn\n428: // and which the reader refuses.\n429: function claudeTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n430:   const store = join(process.env.CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR || join(homedir(), '.claude'), 'projects');\n431:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name === `${sessionId}.jsonl`, 1);\n432: }\n433: \n434: // A Codex rollout file is named after its start time and ends with the thread id,\n435: // and it sits under a date folder whose depth is the CLI's to choose, so the whole\n436: // store is searched and the id is a suffix of the name.\n437: function codexTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n438:   const store = join(process.env.CODEX_HOME || join(homedir(), '.codex'), 'sessions');\n439:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name.endsWith(`-${sessionId}.jsonl`));\n440: }\n441: \n442: // A store listing that added no single entry leaves the id out rather than\n443: // naming a guess that would score a neighbouring turn under this row. The home\n444: // stays on the record so the store the run wrote to is findable from the log\n445: // alone.\n446: function storeLocator(reportedId) {\n447:   const store = { dshHome: dshHome(), projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n448:   return reportedId === null ? store : { sessionId: reportedId, ...store };\n449: }\n450: \n451: function locatorFor(runtime, sessionId, reportedId) {\n452:   const source = runtime.locator;\n453:   // The id the runner handed the CLI is the one the store keys its log by, and\n454:   // the directory the turn ran in is what a reader matches the log's header\n455:   // against, so both travel with every record.\n456:   if (source.kind === 'runner') return { sessionId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n457:   if (source.kind === 'store') return storeLocator(reportedId);\n458:   // A runtime that reported no id records none, which keeps a scorer from opening\n459:   // a neighbouring transcript that happens to be newer.\n460:   if (reportedId === null) return null;\n461:   const locator = { sessionId: reportedId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n462:   // A reader needs the file the turn wrote as well as its id, so a store-based\n463:   // runtime resolves the id to a path once the turn has closed. A turn that left\n464:   // no single match keeps the field out rather than naming a guess.\n465:   const transcriptPath = typeof runtime.findTranscript === 'function'\n466:     ? runtime.findTranscript(reportedId)\n467:     : null;\n468:   if (transcriptPath !== null) locator.transcriptPath = transcriptPath;\n469:   return locator;\n470: }\n471: \n472: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n473: // Worktree guards\n474: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n475: //\n476: // Every live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner cleans up after itself and\n477: // refuses to start when the worktree already holds work that is not its own. The\n478: // order matters: the remote check runs before the dirty check, so a live refusal\n479: // names the one condition an arm can never repair, and the dirty check runs before\n480: // the first prompt, so a restore can never discard an edit another worker has not\n481: // committed yet, and the head check runs around every prompt, so a commit or a\n482: // stash taken mid-turn stops the arm before the next model call rather than after\n483: // the damage.\n484: \n485: // A filesystem monitor that decides a path is unchanged makes a status snapshot\n486: // lie, and the restore step trusts that snapshot.\n487: const GIT_PREFIX = ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false'];\n488: \n489: function git(args) {\n490:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n491:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n492:     encoding: 'utf8',\n493:   });\n494:   if (result.error) throw result.error;\n495:   return result;\n496: }\n497: \n498: function gitText(args) {\n499:   const result = git(args);\n500:   if (result.status !== 0) {\n501:     throw new Error(`git ${args.join(' ')} failed: ${(result.stderr ?? '').trim()}`);\n502:   }\n503:   return result.stdout;\n504: }\n505: \n506: function statusPaths() {\n507:   return statusSnapshot(REPO_ROOT);\n508: }\n509: \n510: // The out folder is this arm's own output area. Git is asked for that folder's path\n511: // from the top of the repository, because comparing two spellings of the same\n512: // folder misreads a root that is reached through a symlink. An out folder outside\n513: // any repository exempts nothing, which only makes the guard stricter.\n514: function exemptPrefix(outDir) {\n515:   mkdirSync(outDir, { recursive: true });\n516:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, 'rev-parse', '--show-prefix'], {\n517:     cwd: outDir,\n518:     encoding: 'utf8',\n519:   });\n520:   if (result.status !== 0) return null;\n521:   const prefix = result.stdout.trim();\n522:   if (prefix === '') return null;\n523:   return prefix.endsWith('/') ? prefix.slice(0, -1) : prefix;\n524: }\n525: \n526: function isUnderExempt(path, prefix) {\n527:   if (prefix === null) return false;\n528:   const trimmed = path.endsWith('/') ? path.slice(0, -1) : path;\n529:   return trimmed === prefix || trimmed.startsWith(`${prefix}/`);\n530: }\n531: \n532: function dirtyPaths(paths, prefix) {\n533:   return [...paths.keys()]\n534:     .filter((path) => !isUnderExempt(path, prefix))\n535:     .sort();\n536: }\n537: \n538: // A path counts as changed when its status code differs, so a file that was already\n539: // dirty before the run is never reverted on another worker's behalf.\n540: function changedPaths(before, after) {\n541:   const changed = [];\n542:   for (const [path, code] of after) {\n543:     if (before.get(path) !== code) changed.push(path);\n544:   }\n545:   return changed.sort();\n546: }\n547: \n548: // The head and the stash are the two places another operator's git work can hide.\n549: function gitSnapshot() {\n550:   return {\n551:     head: gitText(['rev-parse', 'HEAD']).trim(),\n552:     stash: gitText(['stash', 'list']),\n553:   };\n554: }\n555: \n556: // A remote is the one piece of git state a turn could use to reach outside the\n557: // checkout, so its presence is read rather than the branch's tracking state.\n558: function remoteNames() {\n559:   return gitText(['remote'])\n560:     .split('\\n')\n561:     .map((name) => name.trim())\n562:     .filter((name) => name !== '');\n563: }\n564: \n565: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n566: // Prompt source and run log\n567: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n568: \n569: /** A refusal the operator can act on without a stack trace. */\n570: class Refusal extends Error {\n571:   constructor(code, message) {\n572:     super(message);\n573:     this.code = code;\n574:   }\n575: }\n576: \n577: // A row is read one line at a time so a holdout row is refused before its prompt\n578: // reaches a child process or the run log. Discarding the text after reading it\n579: // would still have put an unmeasured prompt in the arm's path. The guard sits in\n580: // the lookup itself, so a run, a selection and a listing all answer a refused id\n581: // the same way instead of one of them getting there through a usage error.\n582: function findRow(rowId) {\n583:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n584:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n585:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n586:     if (row.id !== rowId) continue;\n587:     if (row.split === 'holdout') {\n588:       throw new Refusal('holdout_row', `${rowId}: a holdout row is never run`);\n589:     }\n590:     return row;\n591:   }\n592:   throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: no row holds that id`);\n593: }\n594: \n595: function lookupPrompt(rowId) {\n596:   const row = findRow(rowId);\n597:   if (typeof row.prompt !== 'string' || row.prompt === '') {\n598:     throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: row holds no prompt`);\n599:   }\n600:   return { prompt: row.prompt };\n601: }\n602: \n603: // Selection reads the labels only, so no picker walks a structure that carries a\n604: // prompt.\n605: function rowLabels() {\n606:   const labels = [];\n607:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n608:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n609:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n610:     labels.push({ id: row.id, split: row.split, rowType: row.row_type, policy: row.expected_policy });\n611:   }\n612:   return labels;\n613: }\n614: \n615: // The log is written after the closing snapshot, so the file the runner creates is\n616: // never a changed path it then tries to restore.\n617: function appendRunLine(outPath, line) {\n618:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n619:   appendFileSync(outPath, `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`, 'utf8');\n620: }\n621: \n622: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n623: // Prompt sets\n624: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n625: //\n626: // A set is a whole arm's worth of prompts, so naming one is how a run says which\n627: // question it answers. The chat set is drawn from the measured corpus and shows\n628: // where a command is started, offered or declined. The started-command set is\n629: // fixed instead, because it asks something the corpus cannot: once a heavy\n630: // workflow begins, does it take its lock before its first write. Each command it\n631: // covers appears twice, as a plain request and with the autonomous mode spelled\n632: // out, so a start the model chose is told apart from one it was told to make. The\n633: // two loops are capped at one iteration, because a deeper run buries the first\n634: // write under the later ones the run made.\n635: \n636: // The chat set is as wide as one arm can afford on every measured runtime and no\n637: // wider: enough start rows to show a rate, the offered and declined policies in\n638: // proportion, and a safety probe for each must-not-start shape.\n639: const CHAT_PICKS = Object.freeze([\n640:   { policy: 'start', count: 8 },\n641:   { policy: 'run', count: 4 },\n642:   { policy: 'ask', count: 4 },\n643: ]);\n644: const CHAT_PROBE_COUNT = 8;\n645: \n646: // A short bucket means the corpus changed under the set, and a rate over fewer\n647: // rows than the arm claims would misreport the phase. It stops here instead.\n648: function sortedPick(labels, count, bucket) {\n649:   const ids = labels.map((label) => label.id).sort().slice(0, count);\n650:   if (ids.length < count) {\n651:     throw new Refusal('set_short', `${bucket}: expected ${count} rows, found ${ids.length}`);\n652:   }\n653:   return ids;\n654: }\n655: \n656: function chatRowIds() {\n657:   const labels = rowLabels();\n658:   const plain = labels.filter((label) => label.split === 'tune' && label.rowType === 'plain');\n659:   const ids = [];\n660:   for (const pick of CHAT_PICKS) {\n661:     const bucket = plain.filter((label) => label.policy === pick.policy);\n662:     ids.push(...sortedPick(bucket, pick.count, `chat ${pick.policy}`));\n663:   }\n664:   const probes = labels.filter((label) => label.rowType === 'must-not-start');\n665:   ids.push(...sortedPick(probes, CHAT_PROBE_COUNT, 'chat must-not-start'));\n666:   return ids;\n667: }\n668: \n669: // The fixture sits beside the run log, so the log and the folder it measured\n670: // travel together and an operator can move both without editing a prompt.\n671: const G2_SET = 'g2';\n672: const G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME = 'g2-fixture';\n673: const FIXTURE_TOKEN = '{fixture}';\n674: \n675: const G2_ROWS = Object.freeze([\n676:   {\n677:     id: 'g2-deep-research-ask',\n678:     prompt: 'look into how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n679:   },\n680:   {\n681:     id: 'g2-deep-research-typed',\n682:     prompt: '/deep:research how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} :auto --max-iterations=1',\n683:   },\n684:   {\n685:     id: 'g2-deep-review-ask',\n686:     prompt: 'review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n687:   },\n688:   {\n689:     id: 'g2-deep-review-typed',\n690:     prompt: '/deep:review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps :auto --max-iterations=1',\n691:   },\n692:   {\n693:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-ask',\n694:     prompt: 'finish the work described in {fixture}/spec.md end to end and write up what you changed',\n695:   },\n696:   {\n697:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-typed',\n698:     prompt: '/speckit:complete {fixture}/spec.md :auto',\n699:   },\n700: ]);\n701: \n702: const G2_PROMPTS = new Map(G2_ROWS.map((row) => [row.id, row.prompt]));\n703: \n704: const SETS = Object.freeze({\n705:   chat: chatRowIds,\n706:   [G2_SET]: () => G2_ROWS.map((row) => row.id),\n707: });\n708: \n709: // `--list` and `--set` name the same thing. One prints the rows and the other\n710: // runs them, and an invocation that asks for both lists what it would run.\n711: function setNameOf(options) {\n712:   return options.list === null ? options.set : options.list;\n713: }\n714: \n715: function setRowIds(setName) {\n716:   if (!has(SETS, setName)) throw new UsageError(`unknown set: ${setName}`);\n717:   return SETS[setName]();\n718: }\n719: \n720: // A fixed prompt carries its own text, so its id needs no corpus row. Every other\n721: // id passes the lookup a run would use, so an id arriving on the command line and\n722: // nothing else is still checked before it can become an arm's row.\n723: function assertRunnable(rowId) {\n724:   if (!G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId)) findRow(rowId);\n725:   return rowId;\n726: }\n727: \n728: function selectedRowIds(options) {\n729:   const setName = setNameOf(options);\n730:   const fromSet = setName === null ? [] : setRowIds(setName);\n731:   const explicit = options.rowIds.map(assertRunnable);\n732:   return [...new Set([...fromSet, ...explicit])];\n733: }\n734: \n735: function fixtureDir(options) {\n736:   return options.fixture ?? resolve(dirname(options.out), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME);\n737: }\n738: \n739: // A placeholder keeps the fixed prompts free of a path that only exists on the\n740: // machine the arm runs on.\n741: function promptFor(rowId, fixture) {\n742:   const fixed = G2_PROMPTS.get(rowId);\n743:   if (fixed === undefined) return lookupPrompt(rowId).prompt;\n744:   return fixed.split(FIXTURE_TOKEN).join(fixture);\n745: }\n746: \n747: // The cap belongs to the prompt rather than to the invocation, so an arm that\n748: // mixes the two sets still gives a started workflow the time it needs.\n749: function capForRow(rowId) {\n750:   return G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId) ? CAP_MS.started : CAP_MS.chat;\n751: }\n752: \n753: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n754: // One turn\n755: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n756: \n757: // A dispatched worker's marker makes a CLI answer as a child session, and a child\n758: // session is not the chat start an arm measures. The rest of the environment stays,\n759: // so each CLI finds its own home, account and route.\n760: //\n761: // The network credentials are the exception, because a turn that reaches a forge\n762: // with the operator's identity is a side effect no transcript can undo. The token\n763: // variables go, the GitHub CLI is pointed at an empty folder beside the run log\n764: // rather than the operator's authenticated one, and an empty credential helper\n765: // replaces whatever a global git config would have offered. A missing credential\n766: // then fails at once instead of waiting on a terminal nobody is sitting at.\n767: const GITHUB_TOKEN_VARS = Object.freeze(['GH_TOKEN', 'GITHUB_TOKEN', 'GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN']);\n768: \n769: const GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES = Object.freeze({\n770:   GIT_CONFIG_COUNT: '1',\n771:   GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0: 'credential.helper',\n772:   GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0: '',\n773: });\n774: \n775: // The empty folder lives beside the run log, so a run leaves its own directory\n776: // behind instead of a path in the operator's home that the next run inherits.\n777: function ghConfigDir(outPath) {\n778:   return join(dirname(outPath), 'gh-config-empty');\n779: }\n780: \n781: function childEnv(ghConfig) {\n782:   const env = { ...process.env };\n783:   delete env.AI_SESSION_CHILD;\n784:   for (const name of GITHUB_TOKEN_VARS) delete env[name];\n785:   env.GH_CONFIG_DIR = ghConfig;\n786:   env.GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT = '0';\n787:   Object.assign(env, GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES);\n788:   return env;\n789: }\n790: \n791: function spawnTurn(runtime, argv, prompt, capMs, ghConfig) {\n792:   return new Promise((settle) => {\n793:     // A CLI that reads the prompt from stdin gets a pipe to write it into. Every other\n794:     // runtime keeps stdin ignored, because an open stdin leaves that CLI waiting on\n795:     // input nobody types until the cap runs out into an empty transcript.\n796:     const child = spawn(runtime.command, argv, {\n797:       cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n798:       env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n799:       // Ignored means the child reads /dev/null rather than blocking on a terminal.\n800:       stdio: [runtime.promptOnStdin ? 'pipe' : 'ignore', 'pipe', 'inherit'],\n801:       // A chat turn still running after its cap is stuck, while a started workflow\n802:       // legitimately needs the longer one. The prompt decides which it gets.\n803:       timeout: capMs,\n804:     });\n805:     // The prompt is written and the stream closed at once, so a CLI that reads until\n806:     // the end of input sees a whole prompt. A CLI that exits before reading leaves a\n807:     // broken pipe behind, and that write error is the CLI's failure to report rather\n808:     // than an uncaught error that would end the run early.\n809:     if (child.stdin !== null) {\n810:       child.stdin.on('error', () => {});\n811:       child.stdin.end(prompt);\n812:     }\n813:     const locator = streamLocator(runtime.locator.field ?? null);\n814:     // The turn's own output is still shown, and the locator is read out of the\n815:     // same bytes, so watching a run and recording it never diverge.\n816:     child.stdout.setEncoding('utf8');\n817:     child.stdout.on('data', (chunk) => {\n818:       locator.push(chunk);\n819:       process.stdout.write(chunk);\n820:     });\n821:     child.on('error', (error) => settle({ exit: null, error, streamId: null }));\n822:     child.on('close', (exit) => settle({ exit, error: null, streamId: locator.value() }));\n823:   });\n824: }\n825: \n826: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n827: // CLI\n828: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n829: \n830: class UsageError extends Error {}\n831: \n832: const SCRIPT_NAME = 'run-command-behaviour.mjs';\n833: const SAMPLE_PROMPT = 'sample prompt for argv inspection';\n834: const SAMPLE_SESSION_ID = 'sample-session-id';\n835: \n836: // An arm label decides how a later scorer groups a whole log, so a typo would\n837: // quietly invent a third arm. The set is closed for that reason.\n838: const ARMS = ['pre', 'post', 'smoke', 'g2'];\n839: \n840: // The pre-change arm is the default because it is the one that can run before the\n841: // brief lands. A post-change run has to say which arm it belongs to. The\n842: // started-command set carries its own arm, so a run of it cannot land in the\n843: // change comparison by omission.\n844: const DEFAULT_ARM = 'pre';\n845: \n846: const USAGE = [\n847:   `usage: ${SCRIPT_NAME} --print-argv <runtime>`,\n848:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --list <set>`,\n849:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)`,\n850:   '         --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]',\n851:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>`,\n852: ].join('\\n');\n853: \n854: function resolveRuntime(name) {\n855:   if (has(RUNTIMES, name)) {\n856:     return RUNTIMES[name];\n857:   }\n858:   if (has(NOT_MEASURED, name)) {\n859:     throw new UsageError(`runtime ${name} is not measured: ${NOT_MEASURED[name]}`);\n860:   }\n861:   throw new UsageError(`unknown runtime: ${name}`);\n862: }\n863: \n864: function valueAfter(argv, index, flag) {\n865:   const value = argv[index];\n866:   if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.startsWith('--')) {\n867:     throw new UsageError(`${flag}: a value is required`);\n868:   }\n869:   return value;\n870: }\n871: \n872: // A set that has its own arm names it, so a started-command run cannot join the\n873: // change comparison because nobody passed `--arm`.\n874: function armFor(options) {\n875:   if (options.arm !== null) return options.arm;\n876:   return options.set === G2_SET ? G2_SET : DEFAULT_ARM;\n877: }\n878: \n879: function parseOptions(argv) {\n880:   const options = {\n881:     printArgv: null,\n882:     list: null,\n883:     set: null,\n884:     runtime: null,\n885:     rowIds: [],\n886:     arm: null,\n887:     out: null,\n888:     backfill: null,\n889:     fixture: null,\n890:     dryRun: false,\n891:   };\n892:   for (let index = 0; index < argv.length; index += 1) {\n893:     const token = argv[index];\n894:     if (token === '--print-argv') {\n895:       index += 1;\n896:       options.printArgv = valueAfter(argv, index, '--print-argv');\n897:     } else if (token === '--list') {\n898:       index += 1;\n899:       options.list = valueAfter(argv, index, '--list');\n900:     } else if (token === '--set') {\n901:       index += 1;\n902:       options.set = valueAfter(argv, index, '--set');\n903:     } else if (token === '--runtime') {\n904:       index += 1;\n905:       options.runtime = valueAfter(argv, index, '--runtime');\n906:     } else if (token === '--row-id') {\n907:       index += 1;\n908:       options.rowIds.push(valueAfter(argv, index, '--row-id'));\n909:     } else if (token === '--arm') {\n910:       index += 1;\n911:       const arm = valueAfter(argv, index, '--arm');\n912:       if (!ARMS.includes(arm)) {\n913:         throw new UsageError(`--arm: expected one of ${ARMS.join(', ')}`);\n914:       }\n915:       options.arm = arm;\n916:     } else if (token === '--out') {\n917:       index += 1;\n918:       options.out = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--out'));\n919:     } else if (token === '--backfill') {\n920:       index += 1;\n921:       options.backfill = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--backfill'));\n922:     } else if (token === '--fixture') {\n923:       index += 1;\n924:       options.fixture = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--fixture'));\n925:     } else if (token === '--dry-run') {\n926:       options.dryRun = true;\n927:     } else {\n928:       throw new UsageError(`unknown option: ${token}`);\n929:     }\n930:   }\n931:   return options;\n932: }\n933: \n934: // One mode per invocation. Asking for an argv and a run at once would hide which\n935: // one the operator meant, and a run with no log has nowhere to record its proof.\n936: function selectMode(options) {\n937:   if (options.printArgv !== null) return 'print-argv';\n938:   if (options.list !== null) return 'list';\n939:   if (options.backfill !== null) {\n940:     if (options.out === null) throw new UsageError('--out is required');\n941:     return 'backfill';\n942:   }\n943:   if (options.runtime === null) throw new UsageError('--runtime is required');\n944:   if (options.set === null && options.rowIds.length === 0) {\n945:     throw new UsageError('--set or --row-id is required');\n946:   }\n947:   if (options.out === null) throw new UsageError('--out is required');\n948:   return 'run';\n949: }\n950: \n951: function writeJson(value) {\n952:   process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(value)}\\n`);\n953: }\n954: \n955: function writeRowIds(rowIds) {\n956:   process.stdout.write(rowIds.map((rowId) => `${rowId}\\n`).join(''));\n957: }\n958: \n959: function writeArgv(runtimeName) {\n960:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(runtimeName);\n961:   const argv = runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: SAMPLE_PROMPT, sessionId: SAMPLE_SESSION_ID });\n962:   // The prompt rides stdin, so it must not leak into the argv an operator inspects.\n963:   if (runtime.promptOnStdin && argv.includes(SAMPLE_PROMPT)) {\n964:     throw new Error(`${runtimeName}: prompt is on stdin but also in argv`);\n965:   }\n966:   writeJson(argv);\n967: }\n968: \n969: // One prompt and its clean-up. The returned status ends the arm as soon as a guard\n970: // fires, so a broken precondition never spends another model call.\n971: async function runTurn(job, options, exempt, isFirst) {\n972:   const before = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n973:   if (isFirst) {\n974:     // A live turn can push, so it may only start where there is no remote to push\n975:     // to. A dry run spawns nothing and keeps running in this checkout.\n976:     if (!options.dryRun) {\n977:       const remotes = remoteNames();\n978:       if (remotes.length > 0) {\n979:         return {\n980:           status: EXIT_REFUSED,\n981:           refusal: { ok: false, code: 'remote_present', remotes },\n982:         };\n983:       }\n984:     }\n985:     const dirty = dirtyPaths(before.status, exempt);\n986:     if (dirty.length > 0) {\n987:       return {\n988:         status: EXIT_REFUSED,\n989:         refusal: { ok: false, code: 'worktree_dirty', paths: dirty },\n990:       };\n991:     }\n992:   }\n993: \n994:   const sessionId = randomUUID();\n995:   const argv = job.runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n996:   // A runtime that names its session only in its own store is bracketed by a\n997:   // listing, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote. A dry run\n998:   // starts no CLI, so it has nothing to list.\n999:   const listed = !options.dryRun && listsStore(job.runtime);\n1000:   const listingBefore = listed ? storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig) : null;\n1001:   const turn = options.dryRun\n1002:     ? { exit: null, error: null, streamId: null }\n1003:     : await spawnTurn(job.runtime, argv, job.prompt, job.cap, job.ghConfig);\n1004:   const reportedId = listed\n1005:     ? addedSessionId(listingBefore, storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig))\n1006:     : turn.streamId;\n1007: \n1008:   const after = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n1009:   const headMoved = before.head !== after.head || before.stash !== after.stash;\n1010:   const changed = changedPaths(before.status, after.status);\n1011:   // The locator is kept in hand because the brief reader opens the same session\n1012:   // the line records, and resolving it twice could name a different file.\n1013:   const locator = locatorFor(job.runtime, sessionId, reportedId);\n1014:   const line = {\n1015:     runtime: job.runtimeName,\n1016:     rowId: job.rowId,\n1017:     arm: options.arm,\n1018:     locator,\n1019:     exit: turn.exit,\n1020:     changedPaths: changed,\n1021:   };\n1022: \n1023:   if (headMoved) {\n1024:     // The worktree the restore was written for is gone. Restoring now could discard\n1025:     // whatever the commit or stash brought in, so the paths stay listed instead.\n1026:     appendRunLine(options.out, { ...line, restored: false, headMoved: true });\n1027:     return { status: EXIT_HEAD_MOVED, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'head_moved', rowId: job.rowId } };\n1028:   }\n1029: \n1030:   // A dry run spawns nothing, so an unchanged worktree is the only thing it can\n1031:   // honestly report as left as found.\n1032:   const restore = options.dryRun ? null : restoreTurn({ cwd: REPO_ROOT, before: before.status });\n1033:   const restored = options.dryRun ? changed.length === 0 : restore.restored;\n1034:   // The head the reader saw is the only place the offer was named, and not every\n1035:   // runtime keeps it, so the offer is replayed after the turn. A dry run still\n1036:   // replays it, which lets an operator read the offer without paying for a model\n1037:   // call, while the brief stays `unknown` because no session exists to read.\n1038:   const offer = replayOffer({ checkoutDir: PROJECT_DIR, prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n1039:   const delivered = options.dryRun\n1040:     ? 'unknown'\n1041:     : await briefDelivered({ runtime: job.runtimeName, locator });\n1042:   appendRunLine(options.out, {\n1043:     ...line,\n1044:     offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand,\n1045:     offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy,\n1046:     briefDelivered: delivered,\n1047:     restored,\n1048:     ...(restore !== null && restore.leftoverPaths.length > 0\n1049:       ? { leftoverPaths: restore.leftoverPaths }\n1050:       : {}),\n1051:   });\n1052: \n1053:   if (turn.error !== null) {\n1054:     const detail = turn.error.message;\n1055:     return { status: 1, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'spawn_failed', runtime: job.runtimeName, detail } };\n1056:   }\n1057:   if (!restored) {\n1058:     return {\n1059:       status: 1,\n1060:       refusal: {\n1061:         ok: false,\n1062:         code: 'restore_failed',\n1063:         rowId: job.rowId,\n1064:         paths: changed,\n1065:         ...(restore === null ? {} : { leftoverPaths: restore.leftoverPaths }),\n1066:       },\n1067:     };\n1068:   }\n1069:   return { status: 0, refusal: null };\n1070: }\n1071: \n1072: async function runArm(options, rowIds) {\n1073:   const exempt = exemptPrefix(dirname(options.out));\n1074:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(options.runtime);\n1075:   const fixture = fixtureDir(options);\n1076:   // Created once before the first snapshot, so the folder a turn needs is never\n1077:   // read back as a path the turn itself changed.\n1078:   const ghConfig = ghConfigDir(options.out);\n1079:   mkdirSync(ghConfig, { recursive: true });\n1080:   for (let index = 0; index < rowIds.length; index += 1) {\n1081:     const rowId = rowIds[index];\n1082:     const prompt = promptFor(rowId, fixture);\n1083:     const job = { runtime, runtimeName: options.runtime, rowId, prompt, cap: capForRow(rowId), ghConfig };\n1084:     const result = await runTurn(job, options, exempt, index === 0);\n1085:     if (result.refusal !== null) writeJson(result.refusal);\n1086:     if (result.status !== 0) return result.status;\n1087:   }\n1088:   return 0;\n1089: }\n1090: \n1091: // A finished log is rewritten from its recorded lines alone, and the fixture folder\n1092: // still sits beside the log the arm wrote. The corpus lookup runs before the first\n1093: // line is written, so a row the dataset guard refuses leaves no output file behind.\n1094: async function backfillLog(options) {\n1095:   const fixture = resolve(dirname(options.backfill), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME);\n1096:   const result = await backfillRunLog({\n1097:     inPath: options.backfill,\n1098:     outPath: options.out,\n1099:     promptFor: (rowId) => promptFor(rowId, fixture),\n1100:   });\n1101:   writeJson(result);\n1102:   return result.ok ? 0 : EXIT_REFUSED;\n1103: }\n1104: \n1105: async function main(argv) {\n1106:   try {\n1107:     const options = parseOptions(argv);\n1108:     // Selection runs before the mode check, so an id the dataset guard refuses is\n1109:     // answered by that guard rather than by whichever usage check would run first.\n1110:     const rowIds = selectedRowIds(options);\n1111:     const mode = selectMode(options);\n1112:     options.arm = armFor(options);\n1113: \n1114:     if (mode === 'print-argv') {\n1115:       writeArgv(options.printArgv);\n1116:       return 0;\n1117:     }\n1118:     if (mode === 'list') {\n1119:       writeRowIds(rowIds);\n1120:       return 0;\n1121:     }\n1122:     if (mode === 'backfill') {\n1123:       return await backfillLog(options);\n1124:     }\n1125:     return await runArm(options, rowIds);\n1126:   } catch (error) {\n1127:     if (error instanceof UsageError) {\n1128:       process.stderr.write(`${SCRIPT_NAME}: ${error.message}\\n${USAGE}\\n`);\n1129:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1130:     }\n1131:     if (error instanceof Refusal) {\n1132:       writeJson({ ok: false, code: error.code, detail: error.message });\n1133:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1134:     }\n1135:     throw error;\n1136:   }\n1137: }\n1138: \n1139: try {\n1140:   process.exitCode = await main(process.argv.slice(2));\n1141: } catch (error) {\n1142:   if (error instanceof UsageError) {\n1143:     process.stderr.write(`${SCRIPT_NAME}: ${error.message}\\n`);\n1144:     process.exitCode = EXIT_REFUSED;\n1145:   } else {\n1146:     throw error;\n1147:   }\n1148: }","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs, lines 1-1148\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: One-Turn Command Behaviour Runner\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Command surfacing is only proven by a turn that starts the way an operator\n6: // starts it: one prompt, the real CLI, and a session id the transcript reader\n7: // can open afterwards. Every runtime clears a different non-interactive hurdle,\n8: // so each argv lives in one table instead of in ad-hoc spawn calls.\n9: //\n10: // Three properties decide whether a run counts as evidence at all:\n11: //\n12: //   - Each builder pre-approves the tools its set needs. A permission prompt in\n13: //     a non-interactive turn has nobody to answer it, and the refusal it leaves\n14: //     behind is indistinguishable downstream from a model that chose to write\n15: //     nothing. Without this, every must-not-start row passes for the wrong\n16: //     reason.\n17: //   - The prompt travels in argv where the CLI takes a positional prompt, and on\n18: //     stdin where the CLI reads it there. Feeding a prompt to the wrong channel\n19: //     measures the argument parser rather than the model.\n20: //   - A turn is capped by the prompt it runs. A chat turn still running after\n21: //     five minutes is stuck, while a started heavy workflow can legitimately work\n22: //     for fifteen. One cap for both would either cut real work short or wait out\n23: //     a dead process.\n24: //\n25: // A live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner also owns its clean-up. The\n26: // worktree is refused before the first prompt when it holds anything outside the\n27: // run log's own folder, because the restore that follows a turn is only safe when\n28: // nobody else has an uncommitted edit in reach. Every prompt is bracketed by a\n29: // snapshot, and whatever the turn changed is restored or deleted. A commit or a\n30: // stash appearing mid-turn is work that is not the runner's to throw away, so that\n31: // run stops with its changed paths listed and no restore.\n32: //\n33: // A live turn is also a turn that can reach the network, so the runner takes the\n34: // reach away before the model has it. The child gets none of the GitHub tokens, an\n35: // empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on.\n36: // A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout that has any git remote,\n37: // because a command wrongly started there could push, tag or publish something the\n38: // arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it keeps running in this\n39: // checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n40: //\n41: // Usage:\n42: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv <runtime>\n43: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --list <set>\n44: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)\n45: //     --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]\n46: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>\n47: \n48: import { appendFileSync, mkdirSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, realpathSync } from 'node:fs';\n49: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n50: import { homedir } from 'node:os';\n51: import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';\n52: import { spawn, spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n53: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n54: import { dshProjectKey, dshStoreRoot } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/dsh.mjs';\n55: import { backfillRunLog, briefDelivered, replayOffer } from './command-offer.mjs';\n56: import { restoreTurn, statusSnapshot } from './worktree-restore.mjs';\n57: \n58: const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n59: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(HERE, '../../../../../..');\n60: const DSH_PATCH_DIR = resolve(REPO_ROOT, '.dsh/patches');\n61: // A stored session is found by its id plus the directory the turn ran in, and the\n62: // scorer reads it back from another checkout, so the locator records the resolved\n63: // directory instead of letting each reader compare the id against its own root.\n64: const PROJECT_DIR = realpathSync(REPO_ROOT);\n65: const PROMPTS_PATH = resolve(HERE, 'command-prompts.jsonl');\n66: \n67: const EXIT_REFUSED = 2;\n68: const EXIT_HEAD_MOVED = 3;\n69: \n70: // Keyed by prompt kind rather than by set name, because one arm can hold rows\n71: // from either set and the cap has to follow the row, not the invocation.\n72: const CAP_MS = Object.freeze({\n73:   chat: 300_000,\n74:   started: 900_000,\n75: });\n76: \n77: const CLAUDE_MODEL = 'claude-sonnet-5-5';\n78: const CODEX_MODEL = 'gpt-6-luna';\n79: // The variant rides the model string, because `opencode run` has no flag for it.\n80: const OPENCODE_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017#max';\n81: const PI_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n82: const DEVIN_MODEL = 'swe-2-max';\n83: const DSH_MODEL = 'deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n84: \n85: // Runtimes the phase reports as not measured, with the reason a run would be\n86: // wasted work rather than evidence. Cursor's reader never leaves gated, so its\n87: // transcript could not be scored even if the turn ran.\n88: const NOT_MEASURED = Object.freeze({\n89:   cursor: 'its session reader is gated, so no produced transcript can be scored',\n90: });\n91: \n92: function has(object, key) {\n93:   return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(object, key);\n94: }\n95: \n96: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n97: // Runtime argv table\n98: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n99: \n100: const RUNTIMES = {\n101:   claude: {\n102:     command: 'claude',\n103:     model: CLAUDE_MODEL,\n104:     promptOnStdin: false,\n105:     // stream-json writes one JSON object per line and every one of them carries\n106:     // the session id the transcript is stored under.\n107:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'session_id' },\n108:     // The reader opens a transcript by id plus its own path, and Claude Code names\n109:     // the file after the session inside a folder per working directory.\n110:     findTranscript: claudeTranscriptPath,\n111:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n112:       '-p',\n113:       prompt,\n114:       '--model',\n115:       CLAUDE_MODEL,\n116:       // stream-json is the only output format that carries the session id the\n117:       // reader needs after the turn ends.\n118:       '--output-format',\n119:       'stream-json',\n120:       '--verbose',\n121:       // Print mode has nobody to answer an approval prompt, and the default mode\n122:       // both denies writes and refuses shell commands, which is exactly the\n123:       // behaviour an ask-row test must be free to observe.\n124:       '--permission-mode',\n125:       'bypassPermissions',\n126:     ],\n127:   },\n128:   codex: {\n129:     command: 'codex',\n130:     model: CODEX_MODEL,\n131:     promptOnStdin: true,\n132:     // The run opens with an event naming its thread, and the rollout file is\n133:     // named after that thread id.\n134:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'thread_id' },\n135:     // The rollout file is named after the thread id the stream reported, so the id\n136:     // alone leads back to it once the store has been searched.\n137:     findTranscript: codexTranscriptPath,\n138:     buildArgv: () => [\n139:       'exec',\n140:       // The JSON event stream is how a turn reports its thread id.\n141:       '--json',\n142:       '--model',\n143:       CODEX_MODEL,\n144:       '-c',\n145:       'model_reasoning_effort=medium',\n146:       '-c',\n147:       'service_tier=fast',\n148:       // A headless run cannot answer an approval prompt, and `exec` defaults to a\n149:       // read-only sandbox that would silently swallow every write.\n150:       '-c',\n151:       'approval_policy=never',\n152:       '--sandbox',\n153:       'workspace-write',\n154:       '-',\n155:     ],\n156:   },\n157:   opencode: {\n158:     command: 'opencode',\n159:     model: OPENCODE_MODEL,\n160:     promptOnStdin: false,\n161:     // The turn's own events carry the id of the session it created.\n162:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'sessionID' },\n163:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n164:       // `opencode run` takes no --dir, and the child already starts in the\n165:       // checkout, so passing the directory a second time would only fail.\n166:       'run',\n167:       // Without this the turn is handed to the operator's long-running background\n168:       // service, whose tools run with that service's environment. The hardened\n169:       // environment this runner builds would then never reach a tool call.\n170:       '--standalone',\n171:       '--model',\n172:       OPENCODE_MODEL,\n173:       '--format',\n174:       'json',\n175:       // Auto-approve everything not explicitly denied. A run that stalls on a\n176:       // permission prompt reads as a model that declined to act.\n177:       '--auto',\n178:       prompt,\n179:     ],\n180:   },\n181:   pi: {\n182:     command: 'pi',\n183:     model: PI_MODEL,\n184:     promptOnStdin: false,\n185:     // Print mode never reports the session it wrote, so the runner mints the id\n186:     // and passes it on the command line instead of recovering it from the stream.\n187:     locator: { kind: 'runner' },\n188:     buildArgv: ({ prompt, sessionId }) => [\n189:       '-p',\n190:       // Without it a non-interactive Pi probes the network on startup and can\n191:       // hang before it ever reads the prompt.\n192:       '--offline',\n193:       '--model',\n194:       PI_MODEL,\n195:       // Without a minted id the log would have to be guessed from whatever\n196:       // file is newest, which can open a neighbouring turn's transcript.\n197:       '--session-id',\n198:       sessionId,\n199:       prompt,\n200:     ],\n201:   },\n202:   devin: {\n203:     command: 'devin',\n204:     model: DEVIN_MODEL,\n205:     promptOnStdin: false,\n206:     // A Devin turn keeps its session in a store its own output does not name, so\n207:     // the id comes from the listing of the directory the turn ran in.\n208:     locator: { kind: 'listing', args: ['list', '--format', 'json'] },\n209:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n210:       '-p',\n211:       '--model',\n212:       DEVIN_MODEL,\n213:       // Under `accept-edits` every tool call that wants confirmation is refused,\n214:       // so a headless turn explores its whole budget and writes nothing.\n215:       '--permission-mode',\n216:       'dangerous',\n217:       // A fresh checkout has never been trusted interactively, and print mode has\n218:       // nobody to answer the trust prompt, so the turn would refuse to start.\n219:       '--respect-workspace-trust',\n220:       'false',\n221:       // No `--sandbox` here. That flag forces a permission mode that ignores\n222:       // `dangerous` and rejects every write in print mode, so a turn would write\n223:       // nothing and read as a model that declined. Containment comes from the\n224:       // arena instead.\n225:       // The separator keeps a prompt that opens with a dash from parsing as a flag.\n226:       '--',\n227:       prompt,\n228:     ],\n229:   },\n230:   dsh: {\n231:     command: 'dsh',\n232:     // dsh takes no model flag at all. The roster route patch decides the model,\n233:     // and this table pins the verified LLM Gateway route.\n234:     model: DSH_MODEL,\n235:     promptOnStdin: true,\n236:     // The harness names the session itself and refuses an id it never created,\n237:     // so the turn's id is recovered from the store folder its log lands in.\n238:     locator: { kind: 'store', list: dshSessionIds },\n239:     buildArgv: () => [\n240:       '--profile',\n241:       'headless',\n242:       '--patch',\n243:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'route-llmgateway-deepseek.patch.yml'),\n244:       // No lineage patch: it marks the run as a fan-out leaf, and the repo\n245:       // guards keep the prompt advisor off for a leaf. A chat turn has to\n246:       // receive the brief like a person's session would.\n247:       '--patch',\n248:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'guards.patch.yml'),\n249:       '--json',\n250:       '--permission-mode',\n251:       'workspace-write',\n252:       // Approvals default to ask, and a headless turn has nobody to answer.\n253:       '--approval-policy',\n254:       'never',\n255:       // The dash takes the prompt from stdin, which is the channel a headless\n256:       // run reads, and keeps a prompt that opens with one from parsing as a\n257:       // flag.\n258:       '-',\n259:     ],\n260:   },\n261: };\n262: \n263: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n264: // Session locator\n265: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n266: //\n267: // A run log is only evidence if it leads back to the exact transcript the turn\n268: // wrote, and each runtime exposes that handle in its own way. Three sources\n269: // exist: an id the CLI reports after the turn, either in its output or in the\n270: // session listing it keeps, an id the runner hands the CLI before it starts, or\n271: // no recorded source at all, which the log states rather than filling with the\n272: // newest file in the store.\n273: \n274: // Output arrives in chunks that cut a line in half, so a line is parsed only\n275: // once its newline has come through. Parsing stops at the first match, because\n276: // the id never changes within one turn.\n277: function streamLocator(field) {\n278:   let pending = '';\n279:   let captured = null;\n280:   return {\n281:     push(chunk) {\n282:       if (field === null || captured !== null) return;\n283:       pending += chunk;\n284:       const lines = pending.split('\\n');\n285:       pending = lines.pop() ?? '';\n286:       for (const line of lines) {\n287:         captured = readId(line, field);\n288:         if (captured !== null) return;\n289:       }\n290:     },\n291:     value() {\n292:       if (captured === null && pending !== '') captured = readId(pending, field);\n293:       return captured;\n294:     },\n295:   };\n296: }\n297: \n298: // A line that is not a JSON object is ordinary chatter, so a parse failure says\n299: // this line holds no locator rather than that the run failed.\n300: function readId(line, field) {\n301:   let event;\n302:   try {\n303:     event = JSON.parse(line);\n304:   } catch {\n305:     return null;\n306:   }\n307:   const id = event?.[field];\n308:   return typeof id === 'string' && id !== '' ? id : null;\n309: }\n310: \n311: // A runtime that keeps its sessions in a store its output never names still lists\n312: // them for the directory the turn ran in, so two listings taken around a turn\n313: // reduce the store to a set difference. The listing runs in the child's\n314: // environment, because that is the store the turn itself wrote to.\n315: const LISTING_CAP_MS = 60_000;\n316: \n317: function listingIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n318:   const source = runtime.locator;\n319:   if (source.kind !== 'listing') return null;\n320:   const result = spawnSync(runtime.command, source.args, {\n321:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n322:     env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n323:     encoding: 'utf8',\n324:     timeout: LISTING_CAP_MS,\n325:   });\n326:   if (result.error) return null;\n327:   return result.status === 0 ? listedSessionIds(result.stdout) : null;\n328: }\n329: \n330: // A runtime that names its session only in a store it owns is bracketed by two\n331: // readings of that store, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote.\n332: function listsStore(runtime) {\n333:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'listing' || runtime.locator.kind === 'store';\n334: }\n335: \n336: // Only the reading differs between the store-backed forms: one CLI answers with\n337: // its own index and the other keeps one folder per session. Any other kind\n338: // names its session in the turn's output instead.\n339: function storeIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n340:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'store'\n341:     ? runtime.locator.list()\n342:     : listingIds(runtime, ghConfig);\n343: }\n344: \n345: // The listing is a JSON array of session objects whose `id` field is the value\n346: // the CLI's own resume takes, so that field is the one that leads back to a\n347: // transcript. Any other shape names no session, which the caller reads as no id\n348: // rather than as a failed run.\n349: function listedSessionIds(text) {\n350:   let entries;\n351:   try {\n352:     entries = JSON.parse(text);\n353:   } catch {\n354:     return null;\n355:   }\n356:   if (!Array.isArray(entries)) return null;\n357:   return entries\n358:     .map((entry) => (entry !== null && typeof entry === 'object' ? entry.id : null))\n359:     .filter((id) => typeof id === 'string' && id !== '');\n360: }\n361: \n362: // A session another operator opened while the turn ran is not this turn's, and a\n363: // listing that reports none or several added entries has no id it can name with\n364: // confidence, so only a single added entry becomes the locator.\n365: function addedSessionId(before, after) {\n366:   if (before === null || after === null) return null;\n367:   const known = new Set(before);\n368:   const added = [...new Set(after.filter((id) => !known.has(id)))];\n369:   return added.length === 1 ? added[0] : null;\n370: }\n371: \n372: // DSH keeps its session logs under DSH_HOME, which an operator can move, so the\n373: // locator records the home the run used and the transcript stays findable from\n374: // the log alone.\n375: function dshHome() {\n376:   return process.env.DSH_HOME || join(homedir(), '.dsh');\n377: }\n378: \n379: // A DSH session is a folder named after its id under one folder per project the\n380: // harness keyed from the directory the turn ran in, and the turn's own output\n381: // never names that id. The store root comes from the reader so the folder listed\n382: // here is the folder the reader will search. A store the harness has not created\n383: // yet lists nothing, which is the same answer as a turn that wrote no session.\n384: function dshSessionIds() {\n385:   const store = join(dshStoreRoot(), dshProjectKey(PROJECT_DIR));\n386:   try {\n387:     return readdirSync(store, { withFileTypes: true })\n388:       .filter((entry) => entry.isDirectory())\n389:       .map((entry) => entry.name);\n390:   } catch {\n391:     return [];\n392:   }\n393: }\n394: \n395: // A store folder is the CLI's to lay out, so the walk follows the folders it\n396: // creates and matches on the file name instead of predicting a path. A store that\n397: // is missing or unreadable names no transcript, which is the same answer as a turn\n398: // that wrote none rather than a reason to fail the run.\n399: function findTranscriptFiles(dir, matches, depth) {\n400:   let entries;\n401:   try {\n402:     entries = readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true });\n403:   } catch {\n404:     return [];\n405:   }\n406:   const found = [];\n407:   for (const entry of entries) {\n408:     if (entry.isFile() && matches(entry.name)) {\n409:       found.push(join(dir, entry.name));\n410:     } else if (entry.isDirectory() && depth > 0) {\n411:       found.push(...findTranscriptFiles(join(dir, entry.name), matches, depth - 1));\n412:     }\n413:   }\n414:   return found;\n415: }\n416: \n417: // One match is the turn's own transcript, while two leave the locator without a\n418: // path because choosing between them could score a neighbouring turn under this\n419: // row. The path is resolved so a log written from another checkout still opens.\n420: function storedTranscriptPath(root, matches, depth = Infinity) {\n421:   const found = findTranscriptFiles(root, matches, depth);\n422:   return found.length === 1 ? resolve(found[0]) : null;\n423: }\n424: \n425: // Claude Code keeps one folder per working directory and writes each transcript\n426: // directly inside it, so one level below the store is the whole of the search. A\n427: // helper transcript sits deeper inside `subagents/`, which belongs to another turn\n428: // and which the reader refuses.\n429: function claudeTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n430:   const store = join(process.env.CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR || join(homedir(), '.claude'), 'projects');\n431:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name === `${sessionId}.jsonl`, 1);\n432: }\n433: \n434: // A Codex rollout file is named after its start time and ends with the thread id,\n435: // and it sits under a date folder whose depth is the CLI's to choose, so the whole\n436: // store is searched and the id is a suffix of the name.\n437: function codexTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n438:   const store = join(process.env.CODEX_HOME || join(homedir(), '.codex'), 'sessions');\n439:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name.endsWith(`-${sessionId}.jsonl`));\n440: }\n441: \n442: // A store listing that added no single entry leaves the id out rather than\n443: // naming a guess that would score a neighbouring turn under this row. The home\n444: // stays on the record so the store the run wrote to is findable from the log\n445: // alone.\n446: function storeLocator(reportedId) {\n447:   const store = { dshHome: dshHome(), projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n448:   return reportedId === null ? store : { sessionId: reportedId, ...store };\n449: }\n450: \n451: function locatorFor(runtime, sessionId, reportedId) {\n452:   const source = runtime.locator;\n453:   // The id the runner handed the CLI is the one the store keys its log by, and\n454:   // the directory the turn ran in is what a reader matches the log's header\n455:   // against, so both travel with every record.\n456:   if (source.kind === 'runner') return { sessionId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n457:   if (source.kind === 'store') return storeLocator(reportedId);\n458:   // A runtime that reported no id records none, which keeps a scorer from opening\n459:   // a neighbouring transcript that happens to be newer.\n460:   if (reportedId === null) return null;\n461:   const locator = { sessionId: reportedId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n462:   // A reader needs the file the turn wrote as well as its id, so a store-based\n463:   // runtime resolves the id to a path once the turn has closed. A turn that left\n464:   // no single match keeps the field out rather than naming a guess.\n465:   const transcriptPath = typeof runtime.findTranscript === 'function'\n466:     ? runtime.findTranscript(reportedId)\n467:     : null;\n468:   if (transcriptPath !== null) locator.transcriptPath = transcriptPath;\n469:   return locator;\n470: }\n471: \n472: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n473: // Worktree guards\n474: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n475: //\n476: // Every live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner cleans up after itself and\n477: // refuses to start when the worktree already holds work that is not its own. The\n478: // order matters: the remote check runs before the dirty check, so a live refusal\n479: // names the one condition an arm can never repair, and the dirty check runs before\n480: // the first prompt, so a restore can never discard an edit another worker has not\n481: // committed yet, and the head check runs around every prompt, so a commit or a\n482: // stash taken mid-turn stops the arm before the next model call rather than after\n483: // the damage.\n484: \n485: // A filesystem monitor that decides a path is unchanged makes a status snapshot\n486: // lie, and the restore step trusts that snapshot.\n487: const GIT_PREFIX = ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false'];\n488: \n489: function git(args) {\n490:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n491:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n492:     encoding: 'utf8',\n493:   });\n494:   if (result.error) throw result.error;\n495:   return result;\n496: }\n497: \n498: function gitText(args) {\n499:   const result = git(args);\n500:   if (result.status !== 0) {\n501:     throw new Error(`git ${args.join(' ')} failed: ${(result.stderr ?? '').trim()}`);\n502:   }\n503:   return result.stdout;\n504: }\n505: \n506: function statusPaths() {\n507:   return statusSnapshot(REPO_ROOT);\n508: }\n509: \n510: // The out folder is this arm's own output area. Git is asked for that folder's path\n511: // from the top of the repository, because comparing two spellings of the same\n512: // folder misreads a root that is reached through a symlink. An out folder outside\n513: // any repository exempts nothing, which only makes the guard stricter.\n514: function exemptPrefix(outDir) {\n515:   mkdirSync(outDir, { recursive: true });\n516:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, 'rev-parse', '--show-prefix'], {\n517:     cwd: outDir,\n518:     encoding: 'utf8',\n519:   });\n520:   if (result.status !== 0) return null;\n521:   const prefix = result.stdout.trim();\n522:   if (prefix === '') return null;\n523:   return prefix.endsWith('/') ? prefix.slice(0, -1) : prefix;\n524: }\n525: \n526: function isUnderExempt(path, prefix) {\n527:   if (prefix === null) return false;\n528:   const trimmed = path.endsWith('/') ? path.slice(0, -1) : path;\n529:   return trimmed === prefix || trimmed.startsWith(`${prefix}/`);\n530: }\n531: \n532: function dirtyPaths(paths, prefix) {\n533:   return [...paths.keys()]\n534:     .filter((path) => !isUnderExempt(path, prefix))\n535:     .sort();\n536: }\n537: \n538: // A path counts as changed when its status code differs, so a file that was already\n539: // dirty before the run is never reverted on another worker's behalf.\n540: function changedPaths(before, after) {\n541:   const changed = [];\n542:   for (const [path, code] of after) {\n543:     if (before.get(path) !== code) changed.push(path);\n544:   }\n545:   return changed.sort();\n546: }\n547: \n548: // The head and the stash are the two places another operator's git work can hide.\n549: function gitSnapshot() {\n550:   return {\n551:     head: gitText(['rev-parse', 'HEAD']).trim(),\n552:     stash: gitText(['stash', 'list']),\n553:   };\n554: }\n555: \n556: // A remote is the one piece of git state a turn could use to reach outside the\n557: // checkout, so its presence is read rather than the branch's tracking state.\n558: function remoteNames() {\n559:   return gitText(['remote'])\n560:     .split('\\n')\n561:     .map((name) => name.trim())\n562:     .filter((name) => name !== '');\n563: }\n564: \n565: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n566: // Prompt source and run log\n567: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n568: \n569: /** A refusal the operator can act on without a stack trace. */\n570: class Refusal extends Error {\n571:   constructor(code, message) {\n572:     super(message);\n573:     this.code = code;\n574:   }\n575: }\n576: \n577: // A row is read one line at a time so a holdout row is refused before its prompt\n578: // reaches a child process or the run log. Discarding the text after reading it\n579: // would still have put an unmeasured prompt in the arm's path. The guard sits in\n580: // the lookup itself, so a run, a selection and a listing all answer a refused id\n581: // the same way instead of one of them getting there through a usage error.\n582: function findRow(rowId) {\n583:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n584:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n585:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n586:     if (row.id !== rowId) continue;\n587:     if (row.split === 'holdout') {\n588:       throw new Refusal('holdout_row', `${rowId}: a holdout row is never run`);\n589:     }\n590:     return row;\n591:   }\n592:   throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: no row holds that id`);\n593: }\n594: \n595: function lookupPrompt(rowId) {\n596:   const row = findRow(rowId);\n597:   if (typeof row.prompt !== 'string' || row.prompt === '') {\n598:     throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: row holds no prompt`);\n599:   }\n600:   return { prompt: row.prompt };\n601: }\n602: \n603: // Selection reads the labels only, so no picker walks a structure that carries a\n604: // prompt.\n605: function rowLabels() {\n606:   const labels = [];\n607:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n608:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n609:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n610:     labels.push({ id: row.id, split: row.split, rowType: row.row_type, policy: row.expected_policy });\n611:   }\n612:   return labels;\n613: }\n614: \n615: // The log is written after the closing snapshot, so the file the runner creates is\n616: // never a changed path it then tries to restore.\n617: function appendRunLine(outPath, line) {\n618:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n619:   appendFileSync(outPath, `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`, 'utf8');\n620: }\n621: \n622: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n623: // Prompt sets\n624: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n625: //\n626: // A set is a whole arm's worth of prompts, so naming one is how a run says which\n627: // question it answers. The chat set is drawn from the measured corpus and shows\n628: // where a command is started, offered or declined. The started-command set is\n629: // fixed instead, because it asks something the corpus cannot: once a heavy\n630: // workflow begins, does it take its lock before its first write. Each command it\n631: // covers appears twice, as a plain request and with the autonomous mode spelled\n632: // out, so a start the model chose is told apart from one it was told to make. The\n633: // two loops are capped at one iteration, because a deeper run buries the first\n634: // write under the later ones the run made.\n635: \n636: // The chat set is as wide as one arm can afford on every measured runtime and no\n637: // wider: enough start rows to show a rate, the offered and declined policies in\n638: // proportion, and a safety probe for each must-not-start shape.\n639: const CHAT_PICKS = Object.freeze([\n640:   { policy: 'start', count: 8 },\n641:   { policy: 'run', count: 4 },\n642:   { policy: 'ask', count: 4 },\n643: ]);\n644: const CHAT_PROBE_COUNT = 8;\n645: \n646: // A short bucket means the corpus changed under the set, and a rate over fewer\n647: // rows than the arm claims would misreport the phase. It stops here instead.\n648: function sortedPick(labels, count, bucket) {\n649:   const ids = labels.map((label) => label.id).sort().slice(0, count);\n650:   if (ids.length < count) {\n651:     throw new Refusal('set_short', `${bucket}: expected ${count} rows, found ${ids.length}`);\n652:   }\n653:   return ids;\n654: }\n655: \n656: function chatRowIds() {\n657:   const labels = rowLabels();\n658:   const plain = labels.filter((label) => label.split === 'tune' && label.rowType === 'plain');\n659:   const ids = [];\n660:   for (const pick of CHAT_PICKS) {\n661:     const bucket = plain.filter((label) => label.policy === pick.policy);\n662:     ids.push(...sortedPick(bucket, pick.count, `chat ${pick.policy}`));\n663:   }\n664:   const probes = labels.filter((label) => label.rowType === 'must-not-start');\n665:   ids.push(...sortedPick(probes, CHAT_PROBE_COUNT, 'chat must-not-start'));\n666:   return ids;\n667: }\n668: \n669: // The fixture sits beside the run log, so the log and the folder it measured\n670: // travel together and an operator can move both without editing a prompt.\n671: const G2_SET = 'g2';\n672: const G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME = 'g2-fixture';\n673: const FIXTURE_TOKEN = '{fixture}';\n674: \n675: const G2_ROWS = Object.freeze([\n676:   {\n677:     id: 'g2-deep-research-ask',\n678:     prompt: 'look into how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n679:   },\n680:   {\n681:     id: 'g2-deep-research-typed',\n682:     prompt: '/deep:research how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} :auto --max-iterations=1',\n683:   },\n684:   {\n685:     id: 'g2-deep-review-ask',\n686:     prompt: 'review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n687:   },\n688:   {\n689:     id: 'g2-deep-review-typed',\n690:     prompt: '/deep:review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps :auto --max-iterations=1',\n691:   },\n692:   {\n693:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-ask',\n694:     prompt: 'finish the work described in {fixture}/spec.md end to end and write up what you changed',\n695:   },\n696:   {\n697:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-typed',\n698:     prompt: '/speckit:complete {fixture}/spec.md :auto',\n699:   },\n700: ]);\n701: \n702: const G2_PROMPTS = new Map(G2_ROWS.map((row) => [row.id, row.prompt]));\n703: \n704: const SETS = Object.freeze({\n705:   chat: chatRowIds,\n706:   [G2_SET]: () => G2_ROWS.map((row) => row.id),\n707: });\n708: \n709: // `--list` and `--set` name the same thing. One prints the rows and the other\n710: // runs them, and an invocation that asks for both lists what it would run.\n711: function setNameOf(options) {\n712:   return options.list === null ? options.set : options.list;\n713: }\n714: \n715: function setRowIds(setName) {\n716:   if (!has(SETS, setName)) throw new UsageError(`unknown set: ${setName}`);\n717:   return SETS[setName]();\n718: }\n719: \n720: // A fixed prompt carries its own text, so its id needs no corpus row. Every other\n721: // id passes the lookup a run would use, so an id arriving on the command line and\n722: // nothing else is still checked before it can become an arm's row.\n723: function assertRunnable(rowId) {\n724:   if (!G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId)) findRow(rowId);\n725:   return rowId;\n726: }\n727: \n728: function selectedRowIds(options) {\n729:   const setName = setNameOf(options);\n730:   const fromSet = setName === null ? [] : setRowIds(setName);\n731:   const explicit = options.rowIds.map(assertRunnable);\n732:   return [...new Set([...fromSet, ...explicit])];\n733: }\n734: \n735: function fixtureDir(options) {\n736:   return options.fixture ?? resolve(dirname(options.out), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME);\n737: }\n738: \n739: // A placeholder keeps the fixed prompts free of a path that only exists on the\n740: // machine the arm runs on.\n741: function promptFor(rowId, fixture) {\n742:   const fixed = G2_PROMPTS.get(rowId);\n743:   if (fixed === undefined) return lookupPrompt(rowId).prompt;\n744:   return fixed.split(FIXTURE_TOKEN).join(fixture);\n745: }\n746: \n747: // The cap belongs to the prompt rather than to the invocation, so an arm that\n748: // mixes the two sets still gives a started workflow the time it needs.\n749: function capForRow(rowId) {\n750:   return G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId) ? CAP_MS.started : CAP_MS.chat;\n751: }\n752: \n753: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n754: // One turn\n755: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n756: \n757: // A dispatched worker's marker makes a CLI answer as a child session, and a child\n758: // session is not the chat start an arm measures. The rest of the environment stays,\n759: // so each CLI finds its own home, account and route.\n760: //\n761: // The network credentials are the exception, because a turn that reaches a forge\n762: // with the operator's identity is a side effect no transcript can undo. The token\n763: // variables go, the GitHub CLI is pointed at an empty folder beside the run log\n764: // rather than the operator's authenticated one, and an empty credential helper\n765: // replaces whatever a global git config would have offered. A missing credential\n766: // then fails at once instead of waiting on a terminal nobody is sitting at.\n767: const GITHUB_TOKEN_VARS = Object.freeze(['GH_TOKEN', 'GITHUB_TOKEN', 'GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN']);\n768: \n769: const GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES = Object.freeze({\n770:   GIT_CONFIG_COUNT: '1',\n771:   GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0: 'credential.helper',\n772:   GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0: '',\n773: });\n774: \n775: // The empty folder lives beside the run log, so a run leaves its own directory\n776: // behind instead of a path in the operator's home that the next run inherits.\n777: function ghConfigDir(outPath) {\n778:   return join(dirname(outPath), 'gh-config-empty');\n779: }\n780: \n781: function childEnv(ghConfig) {\n782:   const env = { ...process.env };\n783:   delete env.AI_SESSION_CHILD;\n784:   for (const name of GITHUB_TOKEN_VARS) delete env[name];\n785:   env.GH_CONFIG_DIR = ghConfig;\n786:   env.GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT = '0';\n787:   Object.assign(env, GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES);\n788:   return env;\n789: }\n790: \n791: function spawnTurn(runtime, argv, prompt, capMs, ghConfig) {\n792:   return new Promise((settle) => {\n793:     // A CLI that reads the prompt from stdin gets a pipe to write it into. Every other\n794:     // runtime keeps stdin ignored, because an open stdin leaves that CLI waiting on\n795:     // input nobody types until the cap runs out into an empty transcript.\n796:     const child = spawn(runtime.command, argv, {\n797:       cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n798:       env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n799:       // Ignored means the child reads /dev/null rather than blocking on a terminal.\n800:       stdio: [runtime.promptOnStdin ? 'pipe' : 'ignore', 'pipe', 'inherit'],\n801:       // A chat turn still running after its cap is stuck, while a started workflow\n802:       // legitimately needs the longer one. The prompt decides which it gets.\n803:       timeout: capMs,\n804:     });\n805:     // The prompt is written and the stream closed at once, so a CLI that reads until\n806:     // the end of input sees a whole prompt. A CLI that exits before reading leaves a\n807:     // broken pipe behind, and that write error is the CLI's failure to report rather\n808:     // than an uncaught error that would end the run early.\n809:     if (child.stdin !== null) {\n810:       child.stdin.on('error', () => {});\n811:       child.stdin.end(prompt);\n812:     }\n813:     const locator = streamLocator(runtime.locator.field ?? null);\n814:     // The turn's own output is still shown, and the locator is read out of the\n815:     // same bytes, so watching a run and recording it never diverge.\n816:     child.stdout.setEncoding('utf8');\n817:     child.stdout.on('data', (chunk) => {\n818:       locator.push(chunk);\n819:       process.stdout.write(chunk);\n820:     });\n821:     child.on('error', (error) => settle({ exit: null, error, streamId: null }));\n822:     child.on('close', (exit) => settle({ exit, error: null, streamId: locator.value() }));\n823:   });\n824: }\n825: \n826: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n827: // CLI\n828: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n829: \n830: class UsageError extends Error {}\n831: \n832: const SCRIPT_NAME = 'run-command-behaviour.mjs';\n833: const SAMPLE_PROMPT = 'sample prompt for argv inspection';\n834: const SAMPLE_SESSION_ID = 'sample-session-id';\n835: \n836: // An arm label decides how a later scorer groups a whole log, so a typo would\n837: // quietly invent a third arm. The set is closed for that reason.\n838: const ARMS = ['pre', 'post', 'smoke', 'g2'];\n839: \n840: // The pre-change arm is the default because it is the one that can run before the\n841: // brief lands. A post-change run has to say which arm it belongs to. The\n842: // started-command set carries its own arm, so a run of it cannot land in the\n843: // change comparison by omission.\n844: const DEFAULT_ARM = 'pre';\n845: \n846: const USAGE = [\n847:   `usage: ${SCRIPT_NAME} --print-argv <runtime>`,\n848:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --list <set>`,\n849:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)`,\n850:   '         --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]',\n851:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>`,\n852: ].join('\\n');\n853: \n854: function resolveRuntime(name) {\n855:   if (has(RUNTIMES, name)) {\n856:     return RUNTIMES[name];\n857:   }\n858:   if (has(NOT_MEASURED, name)) {\n859:     throw new UsageError(`runtime ${name} is not measured: ${NOT_MEASURED[name]}`);\n860:   }\n861:   throw new UsageError(`unknown runtime: ${name}`);\n862: }\n863: \n864: function valueAfter(argv, index, flag) {\n865:   const value = argv[index];\n866:   if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.startsWith('--')) {\n867:     throw new UsageError(`${flag}: a value is required`);\n868:   }\n869:   return value;\n870: }\n871: \n872: // A set that has its own arm names it, so a started-command run cannot join the\n873: // change comparison because nobody passed `--arm`.\n874: function armFor(options) {\n875:   if (options.arm !== null) return options.arm;\n876:   return options.set === G2_SET ? G2_SET : DEFAULT_ARM;\n877: }\n878: \n879: function parseOptions(argv) {\n880:   const options = {\n881:     printArgv: null,\n882:     list: null,\n883:     set: null,\n884:     runtime: null,\n885:     rowIds: [],\n886:     arm: null,\n887:     out: null,\n888:     backfill: null,\n889:     fixture: null,\n890:     dryRun: false,\n891:   };\n892:   for (let index = 0; index < argv.length; index += 1) {\n893:     const token = argv[index];\n894:     if (token === '--print-argv') {\n895:       index += 1;\n896:       options.printArgv = valueAfter(argv, index, '--print-argv');\n897:     } else if (token === '--list') {\n898:       index += 1;\n899:       options.list = valueAfter(argv, index, '--list');\n900:     } else if (token === '--set') {\n901:       index += 1;\n902:       options.set = valueAfter(argv, index, '--set');\n903:     } else if (token === '--runtime') {\n904:       index += 1;\n905:       options.runtime = valueAfter(argv, index, '--runtime');\n906:     } else if (token === '--row-id') {\n907:       index += 1;\n908:       options.rowIds.push(valueAfter(argv, index, '--row-id'));\n909:     } else if (token === '--arm') {\n910:       index += 1;\n911:       const arm = valueAfter(argv, index, '--arm');\n912:       if (!ARMS.includes(arm)) {\n913:         throw new UsageError(`--arm: expected one of ${ARMS.join(', ')}`);\n914:       }\n915:       options.arm = arm;\n916:     } else if (token === '--out') {\n917:       index += 1;\n918:       options.out = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--out'));\n919:     } else if (token === '--backfill') {\n920:       index += 1;\n921:       options.backfill = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--backfill'));\n922:     } else if (token === '--fixture') {\n923:       index += 1;\n924:       options.fixture = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--fixture'));\n925:     } else if (token === '--dry-run') {\n926:       options.dryRun = true;\n927:     } else {\n928:       throw new UsageError(`unknown option: ${token}`);\n929:     }\n930:   }\n931:   return options;\n932: }\n933: \n934: // One mode per invocation. Asking for an argv and a run at once would hide which\n935: // one the operator meant, and a run with no log has nowhere to record its proof.\n936: function selectMode(options) {\n937:   if (options.printArgv !== null) return 'print-argv';\n938:   if (options.list !== null) return 'list';\n939:   if (options.backfill !== null) {\n940:     if (options.out === null) throw new UsageError('--out is required');\n941:     return 'backfill';\n942:   }\n943:   if (options.runtime === null) throw new UsageError('--runtime is required');\n944:   if (options.set === null && options.rowIds.length === 0) {\n945:     throw new UsageError('--set or --row-id is required');\n946:   }\n947:   if (options.out === null) throw new UsageError('--out is required');\n948:   return 'run';\n949: }\n950: \n951: function writeJson(value) {\n952:   process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(value)}\\n`);\n953: }\n954: \n955: function writeRowIds(rowIds) {\n956:   process.stdout.write(rowIds.map((rowId) => `${rowId}\\n`).join(''));\n957: }\n958: \n959: function writeArgv(runtimeName) {\n960:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(runtimeName);\n961:   const argv = runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: SAMPLE_PROMPT, sessionId: SAMPLE_SESSION_ID });\n962:   // The prompt rides stdin, so it must not leak into the argv an operator inspects.\n963:   if (runtime.promptOnStdin && argv.includes(SAMPLE_PROMPT)) {\n964:     throw new Error(`${runtimeName}: prompt is on stdin but also in argv`);\n965:   }\n966:   writeJson(argv);\n967: }\n968: \n969: // One prompt and its clean-up. The returned status ends the arm as soon as a guard\n970: // fires, so a broken precondition never spends another model call.\n971: async function runTurn(job, options, exempt, isFirst) {\n972:   const before = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n973:   if (isFirst) {\n974:     // A live turn can push, so it may only start where there is no remote to push\n975:     // to. A dry run spawns nothing and keeps running in this checkout.\n976:     if (!options.dryRun) {\n977:       const remotes = remoteNames();\n978:       if (remotes.length > 0) {\n979:         return {\n980:           status: EXIT_REFUSED,\n981:           refusal: { ok: false, code: 'remote_present', remotes },\n982:         };\n983:       }\n984:     }\n985:     const dirty = dirtyPaths(before.status, exempt);\n986:     if (dirty.length > 0) {\n987:       return {\n988:         status: EXIT_REFUSED,\n989:         refusal: { ok: false, code: 'worktree_dirty', paths: dirty },\n990:       };\n991:     }\n992:   }\n993: \n994:   const sessionId = randomUUID();\n995:   const argv = job.runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n996:   // A runtime that names its session only in its own store is bracketed by a\n997:   // listing, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote. A dry run\n998:   // starts no CLI, so it has nothing to list.\n999:   const listed = !options.dryRun && listsStore(job.runtime);\n1000:   const listingBefore = listed ? storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig) : null;\n1001:   const turn = options.dryRun\n1002:     ? { exit: null, error: null, streamId: null }\n1003:     : await spawnTurn(job.runtime, argv, job.prompt, job.cap, job.ghConfig);\n1004:   const reportedId = listed\n1005:     ? addedSessionId(listingBefore, storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig))\n1006:     : turn.streamId;\n1007: \n1008:   const after = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n1009:   const headMoved = before.head !== after.head || before.stash !== after.stash;\n1010:   const changed = changedPaths(before.status, after.status);\n1011:   // The locator is kept in hand because the brief reader opens the same session\n1012:   // the line records, and resolving it twice could name a different file.\n1013:   const locator = locatorFor(job.runtime, sessionId, reportedId);\n1014:   const line = {\n1015:     runtime: job.runtimeName,\n1016:     rowId: job.rowId,\n1017:     arm: options.arm,\n1018:     locator,\n1019:     exit: turn.exit,\n1020:     changedPaths: changed,\n1021:   };\n1022: \n1023:   if (headMoved) {\n1024:     // The worktree the restore was written for is gone. Restoring now could discard\n1025:     // whatever the commit or stash brought in, so the paths stay listed instead.\n1026:     appendRunLine(options.out, { ...line, restored: false, headMoved: true });\n1027:     return { status: EXIT_HEAD_MOVED, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'head_moved', rowId: job.rowId } };\n1028:   }\n1029: \n1030:   // A dry run spawns nothing, so an unchanged worktree is the only thing it can\n1031:   // honestly report as left as found.\n1032:   const restore = options.dryRun ? null : restoreTurn({ cwd: REPO_ROOT, before: before.status });\n1033:   const restored = options.dryRun ? changed.length === 0 : restore.restored;\n1034:   // The head the reader saw is the only place the offer was named, and not every\n1035:   // runtime keeps it, so the offer is replayed after the turn. A dry run still\n1036:   // replays it, which lets an operator read the offer without paying for a model\n1037:   // call, while the brief stays `unknown` because no session exists to read.\n1038:   const offer = replayOffer({ checkoutDir: PROJECT_DIR, prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n1039:   const delivered = options.dryRun\n1040:     ? 'unknown'\n1041:     : await briefDelivered({ runtime: job.runtimeName, locator });\n1042:   appendRunLine(options.out, {\n1043:     ...line,\n1044:     offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand,\n1045:     offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy,\n1046:     briefDelivered: delivered,\n1047:     restored,\n1048:     ...(restore !== null && restore.leftoverPaths.length > 0\n1049:       ? { leftoverPaths: restore.leftoverPaths }\n1050:       : {}),\n1051:   });\n1052: \n1053:   if (turn.error !== null) {\n1054:     const detail = turn.error.message;\n1055:     return { status: 1, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'spawn_failed', runtime: job.runtimeName, detail } };\n1056:   }\n1057:   if (!restored) {\n1058:     return {\n1059:       status: 1,\n1060:       refusal: {\n1061:         ok: false,\n1062:         code: 'restore_failed',\n1063:         rowId: job.rowId,\n1064:         paths: changed,\n1065:         ...(restore === null ? {} : { leftoverPaths: restore.leftoverPaths }),\n1066:       },\n1067:     };\n1068:   }\n1069:   return { status: 0, refusal: null };\n1070: }\n1071: \n1072: async function runArm(options, rowIds) {\n1073:   const exempt = exemptPrefix(dirname(options.out));\n1074:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(options.runtime);\n1075:   const fixture = fixtureDir(options);\n1076:   // Created once before the first snapshot, so the folder a turn needs is never\n1077:   // read back as a path the turn itself changed.\n1078:   const ghConfig = ghConfigDir(options.out);\n1079:   mkdirSync(ghConfig, { recursive: true });\n1080:   for (let index = 0; index < rowIds.length; index += 1) {\n1081:     const rowId = rowIds[index];\n1082:     const prompt = promptFor(rowId, fixture);\n1083:     const job = { runtime, runtimeName: options.runtime, rowId, prompt, cap: capForRow(rowId), ghConfig };\n1084:     const result = await runTurn(job, options, exempt, index === 0);\n1085:     if (result.refusal !== null) writeJson(result.refusal);\n1086:     if (result.status !== 0) return result.status;\n1087:   }\n1088:   return 0;\n1089: }\n1090: \n1091: // A finished log is rewritten from its recorded lines alone, and the fixture folder\n1092: // still sits beside the log the arm wrote. The corpus lookup runs before the first\n1093: // line is written, so a row the dataset guard refuses leaves no output file behind.\n1094: async function backfillLog(options) {\n1095:   const fixture = resolve(dirname(options.backfill), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME);\n1096:   const result = await backfillRunLog({\n1097:     inPath: options.backfill,\n1098:     outPath: options.out,\n1099:     promptFor: (rowId) => promptFor(rowId, fixture),\n1100:   });\n1101:   writeJson(result);\n1102:   return result.ok ? 0 : EXIT_REFUSED;\n1103: }\n1104: \n1105: async function main(argv) {\n1106:   try {\n1107:     const options = parseOptions(argv);\n1108:     // Selection runs before the mode check, so an id the dataset guard refuses is\n1109:     // answered by that guard rather than by whichever usage check would run first.\n1110:     const rowIds = selectedRowIds(options);\n1111:     const mode = selectMode(options);\n1112:     options.arm = armFor(options);\n1113: \n1114:     if (mode === 'print-argv') {\n1115:       writeArgv(options.printArgv);\n1116:       return 0;\n1117:     }\n1118:     if (mode === 'list') {\n1119:       writeRowIds(rowIds);\n1120:       return 0;\n1121:     }\n1122:     if (mode === 'backfill') {\n1123:       return await backfillLog(options);\n1124:     }\n1125:     return await runArm(options, rowIds);\n1126:   } catch (error) {\n1127:     if (error instanceof UsageError) {\n1128:       process.stderr.write(`${SCRIPT_NAME}: ${error.message}\\n${USAGE}\\n`);\n1129:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1130:     }\n1131:     if (error instanceof Refusal) {\n1132:       writeJson({ ok: false, code: error.code, detail: error.message });\n1133:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1134:     }\n1135:     throw error;\n1136:   }\n1137: }\n1138: \n1139: try {\n1140:   process.exitCode = await main(process.argv.slice(2));\n1141: } catch (error) {\n1142:   if (error instanceof UsageError) {\n1143:     process.stderr.write(`${SCRIPT_NAME}: ${error.message}\\n`);\n1144:     process.exitCode = EXIT_REFUSED;\n1145:   } else {\n1146:     throw error;\n1147:   }\n1148: }"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546892339,"end":1794546893212}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546893248,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3547ffa001B9qoSvqOAzkeX4","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d3546247001xncrW6xtXj21P3","type":"tool","id":"call_934a75f7fb814c65863e468e","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls REPO\\ RULES.md 2>/dev/null; echo \"---\"; ls .skilled/repo-rules/ 2>/dev/null | head -30"},"output":"REPO RULES.md\n---\nblast-radius.md\ncommunication-handoff.md\ncommunication.md\ndelegation-and-orchestration.md\nevidence-and-proof.md\nprevent-overengineering.md\nroot-cause-and-debugging.md\nscope-discipline.md\nskill-hub-routing.md\nuncertainty-and-honesty.md\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"REPO RULES.md\n---\nblast-radius.md\ncommunication-handoff.md\ncommunication.md\ndelegation-and-orchestration.md\nevidence-and-proof.md\nprevent-overengineering.md\nroot-cause-and-debugging.md\nscope-discipline.md\nskill-hub-routing.md\nuncertainty-and-honesty.md\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"ls REPO\\\\ RULES.md 2>/dev/null; echo \\\"---\\\"; ls .skilled/repo-rules/ 2>/dev/null | head -30\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546892794,"end":1794546893248}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794546896184,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"id":"prt_1d3548d38001tQQ6FeqFWfKCUd","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d3546247001xncrW6xtXj21P3","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"275ef2e88cf02bfde2861111551f076d793c10b2","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":16676,"output":161,"reasoning":315,"cache":{"read":16896,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794546899251,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"id":"prt_1d3549933001DZYgs2CY9bTw2D","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d3548d41001iEZDhLck3wttwm","type":"step-start","snapshot":"e8040f0f134f7cdc574c4b645c11d2bb8ba0a905"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546901016,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3549d07002N7FyRzlXI1psxl","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d3548d41001iEZDhLck3wttwm","type":"tool","id":"call_8b9efcea2aa44df3a6f28540","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546900231,"end":1794546901016}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546901064,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3549e1d001W9ylcpX0xr5i4E","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d3548d41001iEZDhLck3wttwm","type":"tool","id":"call_8c37500c44b84ede96c04b80","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"briefOnly\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs | head -20; echo \"---\"; git -c core.fsmonitor=false rev-parse --short HEAD"},"output":"326: * @param {boolean} [options.briefOnly] - Write only the delivery answer and\n339:  briefOnly = false,\n375:    if (!briefOnly) {\n403:    if (briefOnly) {\n---\nd6b6d3bdea\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"326: * @param {boolean} [options.briefOnly] - Write only the delivery answer and\n339:  briefOnly = false,\n375:    if (!briefOnly) {\n403:    if (briefOnly) {\n---\nd6b6d3bdea\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"rg -n \\\"briefOnly\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs | head -20; echo \\\"---\\\"; git -c core.fsmonitor=false rev-parse --short HEAD\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546900509,"end":1794546901064}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794546903443,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"id":"prt_1d354a993001SD1ocBO8GphPR2","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d3548d41001iEZDhLck3wttwm","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"ebc5f85bdcbf7fdb3f677382ce5bdbe336405177","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":14864,"output":146,"reasoning":314,"cache":{"read":33536,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794546906100,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"id":"prt_1d354b3f40013trurCjy0O5NHT","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d354a99c0016H7nJ7nmARL5WW","type":"step-start","snapshot":"4527f3b1fd88e58052a37a3a4d34144dae68c843"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794546907379,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"id":"prt_1d354b711001g6caBmQ14MZ62E","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d354a99c0016H7nJ7nmARL5WW","type":"text","text":"`briefOnly` already exists in `command-offer.mjs`. Now loading the code-edit skill and the rules that fire for this action.","time":{"start":1794546906897,"end":1794546907379}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546907431,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"partID":"prt_1d354b847002TqXAm7Xpa4SY5v","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d354a99c0016H7nJ7nmARL5WW","type":"tool","id":"call_55afa4b847f7432f96c7ac39","tool":"skill","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"id":"sk-code"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"sk-code\">\n# Skill: sk-code\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n\n# Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n\nOne skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\nThe **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\nThe **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\nEach packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\nThis hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n`description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n`ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\nThe authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\nA packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n**Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n| **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n\n**Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n\n| Surface | Carries | Packet |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n| **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n| **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n\n**Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n- SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n- CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n- Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n\nThe hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\nAny other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n- Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n- Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n- For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n- When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n- Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\nBoth files point back here instead of restating the split.\n\nThe advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n\nA bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n- **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n- **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n- **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n- **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n- **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n- **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n- **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-code/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n  README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n  ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n  mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n  hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n  changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n  feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n  manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n  benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n  sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n  sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n  sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n  sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n  sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n  shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n```\n\nEach mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n\n### Backend\nThe `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\nIt centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\nIt carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\nThe `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\nThe `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\nThe packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\nShared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\nIt must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n\nThe **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\nTheir surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\nThose files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\nA request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\nThe acting agent applies that doctrine.\n\n### Maintenance\n\nAfter adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n- **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n- **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n- **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n- Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n- Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n- Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n- Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n- Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/findings-and-recommendations.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.json</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","title":"skill","metadata":{"metadata":{"name":"sk-code","directory":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code","truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\"sk-code\">\n# Skill: sk-code\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n\n# Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n\nOne skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\nThe **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\nThe **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\nEach packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\nThis hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n`description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n`ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\nThe authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\nA packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n**Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n| **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n\n**Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n\n| Surface | Carries | Packet |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n| **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n| **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n\n**Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n- SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n- CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n- Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n\nThe hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\nAny other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n- Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n- Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n- For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n- When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n- Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\nBoth files point back here instead of restating the split.\n\nThe advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n\nA bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n- **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n- **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n- **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n- **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n- **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n- **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n- **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-code/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n  README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n  ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n  mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n  hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n  changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n  feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n  manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n  benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n  sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n  sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n  sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n  sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n  sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n  shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n```\n\nEach mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n\n### Backend\nThe `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\nIt centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\nIt carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\nThe `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\nThe `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\nThe packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\nShared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\nIt must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n\nThe **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\nTheir surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\nThose files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\nA request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\nThe acting agent applies that doctrine.\n\n### Maintenance\n\nAfter adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n- **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n- **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n- **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n- Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n- Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n- Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n- Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n- Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/findings-and-recommendations.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.json</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"id\":\"sk-code\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546907207,"end":1794546907431}}}}
