{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546918400,"sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","part":{"partID":"prt_1d354e16d002C1MVMkYHFnAzE0","sessionID":"ses_e2cad0faaffebn7YTy42utMRho","messageID":"msg_1d354d2e5001R3x9kSTQJHHYTx","type":"tool","id":"call_fcab645cb580467880f3343d","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"defer\" -A 12 .opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md | head -60"},"output":"449:- It defers `assets/*` (checklists, recipes, templates) to on-demand rather than the first slice.\n450-- Within SKILLED it slices once more by the **detected language** (§1 sub-detection): a TypeScript task loads `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/*` plus the language-agnostic `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`, not the Python, shell, config, or JavaScript folders.\n451-- An `UNKNOWN` surface falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map.\n452-\n453-This is what stops a routine single-surface task from pulling the full cross-surface set. The deterministic router-replay enforces the same rule, so the benchmark measures it.\n454-\n455----\n456-\n457-## 9. HOW TO READ THIS\n458-\n459-- Every code task first loads the always-on preamble (stack + phase detection and the universal quality baseline); routing then narrows to the detected surface's slice for the matched intents.\n460-- One dominant intent on one surface loads that surface's slice; two near-tied intents (within the router's ambiguity delta) load both intent sets, deduped by canonical pair.\n461-- A single-surface task never pulls another surface's resources.\n--\n483:> Follow the returned decision: `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate) or `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-code`. Set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0`, the explicit kill-switch, to force legacy routing fleet-wide.\n484-\n485-**Session surface hint.** Before you call the front door, run the surface detection in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md) on the session's working directory and the files it is editing.\n486-Then add its result as `--surface-hint <SURFACE>`, for example `--surface-hint SKILLED`.\n487-When the prompt's keywords tie between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads them.\n488-Workflow modes keep first place, and the targets and the bundle kind stay the same.\n489-`SKILLED` is the fallback surface and always ranks last, so a `SKILLED` hint changes nothing.\n490-With `UNKNOWN`, no hint or a surface the prompt did not match, the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order applies.\n491-\n492-### Surface Router: per-intent leaf sets\n493-\n494-Root `ROUTER.md` owns the second-stage `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP`, while packet-owned resources stay typed through `leaf-manifest.json`.\n495-The hub-level shared controls are declared once, in `ROUTER.md` §8: the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list plus the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble. They resolve on disk but never project as leaves.\n--\n520:When no workflow mode dominates (a bare implement/debug/verify request), the router defers to a pure surface bundle: it detects the surface, loads that surface's evidence and workflow doctrine, and the agent acts. `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`, so the hub does not force a stale default. An unclear code intent asks for disambiguation.\n521-\n522-Per-mode behavior is **not flattened**: each packet keeps its own code-work contract, standards, evidence rules, and tool-permission guards.\n523-\n524-### Routing rule\n525-```python\n526-from pathlib import Path\n527-\n528-SKILL_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n529-UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST = [\n530-    \"Confirm whether this is sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend or sk-code-skilled work\",\n531-    \"Confirm the target files or runtime surface\",\n532-    \"Confirm the expected action: implement, debug, verify, quality gate, or review\",\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"449:- It defers `assets/*` (checklists, recipes, templates) to on-demand rather than the first slice.\n450-- Within SKILLED it slices once more by the **detected language** (§1 sub-detection): a TypeScript task loads `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/*` plus the language-agnostic `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`, not the Python, shell, config, or JavaScript folders.\n451-- An `UNKNOWN` surface falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map.\n452-\n453-This is what stops a routine single-surface task from pulling the full cross-surface set. The deterministic router-replay enforces the same rule, so the benchmark measures it.\n454-\n455----\n456-\n457-## 9. HOW TO READ THIS\n458-\n459-- Every code task first loads the always-on preamble (stack + phase detection and the universal quality baseline); routing then narrows to the detected surface's slice for the matched intents.\n460-- One dominant intent on one surface loads that surface's slice; two near-tied intents (within the router's ambiguity delta) load both intent sets, deduped by canonical pair.\n461-- A single-surface task never pulls another surface's resources.\n--\n483:> Follow the returned decision: `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate) or `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-code`. Set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0`, the explicit kill-switch, to force legacy routing fleet-wide.\n484-\n485-**Session surface hint.** Before you call the front door, run the surface detection in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md) on the session's working directory and the files it is editing.\n486-Then add its result as `--surface-hint <SURFACE>`, for example `--surface-hint SKILLED`.\n487-When the prompt's keywords tie between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads them.\n488-Workflow modes keep first place, and the targets and the bundle kind stay the same.\n489-`SKILLED` is the fallback surface and always ranks last, so a `SKILLED` hint changes nothing.\n490-With `UNKNOWN`, no hint or a surface the prompt did not match, the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order applies.\n491-\n492-### Surface Router: per-intent leaf sets\n493-\n494-Root `ROUTER.md` owns the second-stage `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP`, while packet-owned resources stay typed through `leaf-manifest.json`.\n495-The hub-level shared controls are declared once, in `ROUTER.md` §8: the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list plus the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble. They resolve on disk but never project as leaves.\n--\n520:When no workflow mode dominates (a bare implement/debug/verify request), the router defers to a pure surface bundle: it detects the surface, loads that surface's evidence and workflow doctrine, and the agent acts. `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`, so the hub does not force a stale default. An unclear code intent asks for disambiguation.\n521-\n522-Per-mode behavior is **not flattened**: each packet keeps its own code-work contract, standards, evidence rules, and tool-permission guards.\n523-\n524-### Routing rule\n525-```python\n526-from pathlib import Path\n527-\n528-SKILL_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n529-UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST = [\n530-    \"Confirm whether this is sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend or sk-code-skilled work\",\n531-    \"Confirm the target files or runtime surface\",\n532-    \"Confirm the expected action: implement, debug, verify, quality gate, or review\",\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"rg -n \\\"defer\\\" -A 12 .opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md | head -60\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546917741,"end":1794546918400}}}}
